{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:04:23.298Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Since the SDK is large and slow to import, I'm considering marking it as a server external package in next.config and dynamically importing it inside the agent module so other routes avoid the cost. I also want to verify node:sqlite compatibility with the Next build and check the docs for the right config option in this version.\n\nNow I'm working out the \"type it in\" fix: defining a main field per doc type and an isDocAdded helper so docs without fields count as added once tapped, while docs needing text still keep a pending \"typing\" state until the user fills it in on the confirm screen. I'm adjusting DocsStep to show \"added\" based on isDocAdded, with a distinct status message for docs still awaiting typed details, and updating the check-run logic accordingly.\n\nI'm also weighing whether this should affect engine semantics — deciding it's better to keep the fix client-side, since applying it in the API path would change how AI-extracted docs with failed name reads are treated, which could misleadingly mark a submitted photo as \"missing\" even though the user did add something.\n\nNow moving to T3 storage design: using node:sqlite in a server store module, but I'm concerned about Turbopack and vitest compatibility with node: builtins, so I want to verify that quickly before committing to the approach.\n\nI'm designing the store API with an injectable path for tests (`:memory:`), a singleton getter reading from env or defaulting to `data/kaagaz.db`, and a `rule_versions` table schema tracking scheme id, version, rule/test JSON, status, timestamps, and a kind/baseVersion field to support diffing between new schemes and new versions. I'm also thinking through uniqueness constraints on publish to avoid duplicate scheme+version rows in the registry.\n\nI'm now sketching the store methods (saveProposal, listVersions, getVersion, publish, reject, listPublished) and resolving how built-in schemes fit into the version history -- treating built-ins as the baseline \"version 1\" entry without renumbering them, since that would break existing tests and provenance, instead labeling them \"built-in (2.0.0)\" in the UI history.\n\nFor registry sync, I'm worried that Next.js might load route handlers and server components in separate module instances, meaning a publish via an API route could register a rule in a different registry instance than the one RSC pages use. The fix is to treat the database as the source of truth and have every consumer call an ensureRulesLoaded() function that syncs unregistered published rows on each request.\n\nWith cacheComponents enabled though, page.tsx would be statically prerendered at build time unless I explicitly mark the DB read as dynamic using something like `await connection()` wrapped in Suspense, so I need to check the Next.js docs on that behavior. I'm also noting the admin page can stay static since it's just rendering a client component that fetches from the API, but the citizen-facing catalog page needs to be dynamic so published versions take effect immediately.\n\nFor the agent module, I'm structuring it under `src/server/agents/ruleMaintenance.ts` per the spec, with tool implementations split into a separate `ruleTools.ts` file built via `createRuleTools(deps)` so they're testable independently of the SDK, covering schema retrieval, current rule lookup, proposing rules, running tests, and saving proposals.\n\nFor testing the agent itself, I'm considering mocking it by constructing a custom AgentModel that streams scripted tool-call events, letting me exercise the real SDK loop offline without hitting a live model. The concern is that importing `@cline/sdk` in vitest is slow since it loads the whole core, so I'm weighing whether there's a lighter way to pull in just `Agent` and `createTool`.\n\nNow I'm sketching the pure test case schema in `src/core/ruleTests.ts`, defining fields for both \"benefit\" and \"evaluate\" test kinds — things like date, expected totals, override flags, extracted docs, answers, and as-of dates.\n\nI'm refining this into a cleaner discriminated union, separating \"benefit\" and \"evaluate\" into distinct shapes with their own `expect` sub-objects, each requiring at least one expectation. Then I'm planning `runRuleTests` as a pure function that takes a rule and test cases and returns per-case results flagged as new or regression tests, alongside per-scheme regression suites.\n\nNow I'm working out where built-in regression tests come from -- I need a core regression module with hardcoded cases per scheme, plus published proposals' test sets folded into regression for future versions. But I realize there's a bug: a date-specific benefit test like \"1250 on 2026-10-10\" would break once an October override of 1500 is introduced, so I need to be careful that built-in regression dates avoid override periods.\n\nI'll design benefit regression checks using pre-change dates like 2026-09-01, and keep eligibility/readiness checks separate from benefit-amount checks so genuine policy changes don't spuriously break old tests. I'm also considering how newly added schemes with no prior regression history should be handled.\n\nI'm designing validateProposal as a pure function: it parses against the schema for zod issues, checks that version numbers increase for existing schemes, ensures scheme/question ids don't collide, and requires a sourceUrl for any rule marked verified. I'm also adding a guard against unsafe regex patterns in fieldChecks by capping pattern length, and planning a diff utility to compare rule versions.\n\nFor the diff function, I'm keying array comparisons by id/docType/from when available (falling back to index) so paths read naturally, and for brand-new schemes I'll diff against null and flatten everything into leaf-level \"added\" entries, even though that could produce dozens of rows the UI will need to handle gracefully.\n\nNow I'm designing the rule agent's server flow: a runRuleAgent function that takes a notice string and options (event callback, injectable dependencies, abort signal) and returns an outcome, with tools built via closures that track session state like the last saved proposal id and a versioned system prompt.\n\nBut I realize there's a tension with rule 3, which requires every AI/model call to go through a single module, ai.ts — yet the agent itself calls the model via the SDK. To resolve this, I'll make ai.ts the sole place that constructs model-backed agents, reading the relevant environment config and logging model, prompt version, and an input hash, while ruleMaintenance.ts stays responsible for defining the tools and prompt and simply calls into ai.ts's wrapper to run the tool agent.\n\nStructured output gets handled through tool inputs validated against zod schemas, satisfying the schema-validation requirement, and I'll build in exactly one retry: if the run fails or finishes without saving a valid proposal, nudge it once to continue, then fall back safely to an error object if it still fails.\n\nTo avoid ai.ts eagerly loading the SDK and slowing down unrelated routes and tests, I'll dynamically import the SDK inside the function rather than statically, with an injectable dependency for createAgent so tests can mock it cleanly.\n\nI also need to verify that dynamic SDK imports work properly under Next's bundling — likely requiring a serverExternalPackages config so these packages are required at runtime by Node instead of getting bundled, which I should confirm against the docs.\n\nThen there's the question of whether zod schemas with refinements, defaults, and custom date validation convert cleanly to JSON schema via createTool, since refinements are typically dropped in JSON schema generation — I want to confirm the underlying conversion library handles zod 4 schemas correctly rather than just simple cases.\n\nFor the proposeRule tool specifically, I'm leaning toward a loose input schema that accepts an unvalidated record, then running our own SchemeRule.safeParse validation afterward so the model gets detailed field-level error feedback instead of an opaque tool-level failure.\n\nI should check whether z.toJSONSchema handles refined custom types correctly, since refinements get dropped—I'll need to test this with the IsoDate refine. For the actual workflow, saveProposal must independently re-validate and re-run all tests in code rather than trusting the model's claims, only persisting once everything genuinely passes.\n\nsaveProposal should reject invalid rules or failing tests outright, returning the specific failures, and should require a minimum number of new regression tests (likely 3, matching the done-when criteria). The stored test results get computed by code at save time with status \"proposed,\" while publish needs to re-check things like whether the proposal is still in proposed status and whether the version is already registered, since the regression suite could have shifted if another version was published in the meantime.\n\nFor versioning, I shouldn't let the agent pick arbitrary numbers — validation should enforce strictly increasing versions, like bumping from the current version to something like \"1.0.0\" for a new scheme.\n\nOn provenance, the spec says status should default to provisional unless an official URL backs it — I need a code-level rule that if a proposal claims \"verified\" status, the sourceUrl must actually appear verbatim in the cited notice text, otherwise it gets forced back to provisional.\n\nThis guards against hallucinated URLs. I'll define the allowed set of URLs as those appearing either in the current notice or already present in the existing rule for that scheme — a verified or provisional-with-URL provenance can only cite from that allowed set, never invent something new.\n\nSeparately, I'm weighing the practical cost of requiring the model to echo the entire ~8KB rule JSON three times when proposing a new version. It's token-heavy but tolerable, though there's a real risk the model subtly mangles Assamese characters while copying. Since the diff will surface any accidental changes, that risk is at least detectable, but I should think about whether a patch-style update format would be safer than full-rule copying.\n\nI'm leaning toward letting rule/tests default to the last ones passed in prior calls, documenting that behavior in the tool descriptions. I'm also considering letting proposeRule accept a JSON merge patch against a base scheme instead of a full rule, which would keep payloads small for override-style edits while still requiring a full rule for entirely new schemes.\n\nI also realize the agent will need to write Assamese for new names and notes, which should be flagged as a model-drafted and unverified. It might be worth adding a deterministic validation check that flags use of the Bengali র instead of the correct Assamese ৰ character.\n\nFor test regression, published tests from earlier versions should remain valid unless a later version explicitly changes that rule — that seems fine as a policy. Thinking through sample evaluate-type tests for IGNOAPS: age 65 with BPL should be likely_eligible, age 55 should be not_eligible, and missing aadhaar should trigger action_required readiness — though I need to double check the age range since IGNOAPS covers 60+ with no upper bound.\n\nSince the Benefit model can't represent age-banded amounts, I'd have the agent set the base central share at 200 with a note flagging the higher 80+ amount, leaving state top-up null since it's state-discretion language. For documents, aadhaar serves as age proof alongside BPL proof and bank passbook, with name cross-checks against aadhaar and the passbook, plus a BPL household question as a requirement. No samples apply here. Now I'm moving on to the admin API routes.\n\nI need a propose endpoint that accepts a notice and streams NDJSON tool/text/done events back as a readable stream, a GET endpoint returning all scheme rule versions with diffs and test results, and publish/reject endpoints — and I should double-check how Next 16 handles dynamic route params as promises before wiring these up.\n\nThese admin endpoints have no auth right now, which is risky since anyone could publish rules. For the hackathon I'll add an optional `ADMIN_TOKEN` gate — require an `x-admin-token` header if the env var is set, otherwise allow access and flag it as an open issue rather than build a full token UI flow. I'll also note the cost/rate-limiting concern on the propose endpoint as a log-only observation rather than implementing it now, and keep in mind the agent run itself takes 30-120 seconds so the route handler needs to stream properly during that time.\n\nI should cap the agent with a max iteration count (around 16) and an overall timeout around 180s using an abort signal, and have the UI consume the live tool-call log via an NDJSON stream reader. For the admin page structure, I'll use a static server shell with a client component underneath for interactivity, and since the brief calls for all fixed UI strings to live in one i18n file, I'll put the admin-only strings under their own key there in English, since bilingual admin copy isn't worth the overhead for a maintainer-only tool.\n\nNow for the demo notice buttons, I need to load the two docs/ files server-side. I'm thinking of creating a `src/server/demoNotices.ts` that reads the files via fs at the project root, which should work fine for a static prerender, though I need to double check how that interacts with cacheComponents mode and whether an uncached fs read during prerender causes issues.\n\nFile system reads aren't tracked as dynamic, so this should be safe. I need to strip boilerplate from each doc and only extract the content between the `---` markers, excluding the \"Expected agent behaviour\" section since that would reveal the answer to the agent. For the IGNOAPS doc, there's a source URL mentioned in italics before the `---` that matters for provenance, so I need a careful extraction function that decides what to keep for each doc.\n\nI'm finishing the trim function and testing it, then thinking through whether vikaspedia counts as an \"official\" source—deciding code can't judge officialness so I'll just require a URL be present for verified status, consistent with existing packs. Then I'm sketching the diff UI with colored before/after entries, grouped test results, publish/reject buttons, and a version history view per scheme with status badges.\n\nI realize the home page and the check API both need to sync published rules before reading scheme data, so I'll centralize that in a rules module, and make sure tests use an in-memory DB path so they don't touch real data. I confirm the catalog and benefit calculations will reflect the newly published scheme version correctly.\n\nI still need to check the Next.js docs on serverExternalPackages, cacheComponents, dynamic route params, and streaming responses, plus verify whether node:sqlite works cleanly in both vitest and the Next build. For agent testing, I'm leaning toward mocking the agent with a fake sequential tool-executor to avoid slow SDK imports, though I'm considering one real-SDK integration test with a scripted model to validate tool schemas and event mapping, despite the cold-import time cost—I want to measure that import time now.\n\nThe SDK runs successfully against the Cline API, with a live Agent call completing a createTool invocation in about 2.6s. Next I'll check the SDK's import time for testing purposes, verify node:sqlite loads under Vitest, and review the Next 16 docs.\n\n"}}
