{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:08:46.922Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm thinking through how to fix the \"type it in\" logic so it only counts once the main field actually has a value, considering whether this needs a helper like isDocAdded(doc) in flow.ts that checks DOC_FIELDS per document type, while also noting presence-only documents like death certificates don't fit this model the same way.\n\nI should use missingRequired counts based on isDocAdded, and make sure CoverSheet and ConfirmStep reflect added docs consistently, including photo-derived blank docs that need a name before counting. I'll add i18n strings like \"Type the name on the next screen to add it\" for the draft state, then move to T3: checking that node:sqlite works with Turbopack bundling in a Next.js build.\n\nFor heavy packages like @cline/sdk, I'm considering adding serverExternalPackages for @cline/sdk, @cline/core, @cline/agents, @cline/llms, and @cline/shared since @cline/sdk pulls in the heavy @cline/core module, while still importing Agent from @cline/sdk per the installed dependency rules.\n\nI'm planning to dynamically import @cline/sdk inside the agent runner factory so tests and the tools module don't trigger its load, only loading it when a route actually proposes an action. For the database, I'll store it at data/kaagaz.db, gitignore the data directory, and configure the path via a KAAGAZ_DB_PATH env var while tests use an in-memory store, with a store module exposing openStore and a singleton getStore, including a rule_versions table.\n\nFor uniqueness, I realize rejected proposals might get re-proposed with the same version, so instead of a plain unique constraint on scheme_id and version, I'll use a partial unique index that excludes rejected rows, which SQLite supports, while also being careful not to collide with built-in versions like orunodoi-3 2.0.0.\n\nOn the \"built-ins are version 1\" spec language, I'm interpreting this as meaning built-ins should be seeded as the first published row in the history table, using their actual version string like \"2.0.0\" with a source note marking them as the built-in pack, so the history view treats them as history entry one. I need to figure out where existing regression tests come from for a scheme, since the task requires running new cases plus all existing regression tests together, which means there needs to be a declarative test store per scheme.\n\nI'm defining a schema for individual rule test cases, capturing inputs like the as-of date, extracted docs, and answers, alongside expected outputs covering eligibility, readiness, benefit totals and breakdowns, override flags, and a checks map.\n\nI'm also planning `runRuleTests` as a pure function that evaluates a rule against a list of cases and reports pass/fail results with details, and sketching built-in regression tests for schemes like pm-kisan, nsap-widow, and orunodoi-3 -- being careful that a test expecting a benefit of 1250 doesn't accidentally land on a date after the override takes effect in October 2026, which would bump the benefit to 1500 and break the test.\n\nFor Orunodoi I'm choosing September 15, 2026 as the as-of date for the base benefit test, and reasoning through eligibility logic for provisional exclusions and ration card checks. Rather than guessing at exact outcomes, it's better to actually run the engine to derive the expected results and then pin them in regression tests, since IGNOAPS doesn't have existing tests to compare against.\n\nI'm now thinking about how to structure the regression suite: running tests scoped to the affected scheme ID should be sufficient since other schemes are unaffected by adding a new one. For persisting tests across published versions, I need to decide whether tests_json stores only new cases per version or accumulates as a deduped union across all versions of a scheme, since older test cases should still be checked against newer versions.\n\nFor keeping the registry in sync with the store, I'm thinking through a sync routine that loads each published scheme into the registry on startup and after publishing, guarding against duplicate id+version registration errors.\n\nI should enforce version ordering at proposal time: new versions of existing schemes must have a strictly greater semver than the current published one, while new schemes can start anywhere (suggesting 1.0.0). I also need to make sure the store and registry are initialized before page.tsx or handleCheck run, so I'll add an ensureSchemesLoaded() helper that seeds built-ins and syncs the registry, calling it from both entry points.\n\nBut I realize page.tsx is statically prerendered at build time, which conflicts with needing published scheme updates to show up immediately — the page needs to be dynamic instead. That likely means calling connection() before reading the store, but with cacheComponents that requires wrapping the dynamic part in a Suspense boundary to avoid the \"uncached data accessed outside Suspense\" build error, so I'll restructure the page to have an async Catalog component inside a Suspense wrapper and check the docs for the right pattern.\n\nI'm also worried the registry's module-level state might not behave as a true singleton across route handlers and pages, since Turbopack's production runtime could evaluate shared chunks differently depending on how entries are bundled, so I need to think through whether the DB connection object stays single-instance across the app.\n\nGiven that uncertainty, the safer approach is making `ensureSchemesLoaded()` idempotent and cheap, calling it from every consumer since the DB is the real source of truth—duplicate calls are harmless and only cost a single SELECT. I'll also cache the store on `globalThis` to avoid creating redundant DatabaseSync connections across module instances and to survive dev HMR, relying on WAL mode to handle any concurrent connections to the same file safely, with store.ts remaining the sole writer.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the store's public functions: listing and fetching rule versions, pulling published rules for registry sync, getting regression tests per scheme, saving new proposals with validation against the scheme and test schemas (rejecting duplicates via the unique index), and publishing a proposed version within a transaction while considering whether to rerun tests at publish time.\n\nI realize publish should actually re-run tests against the current regression suite before finalizing, since the suite may have changed since the proposal was saved -- failing tests should return a 409. That logic belongs in a separate service module rather than the store itself, and the version number must still exceed the currently published one. I'm also adding a reject function, defining a status column with proposed/published/rejected states, and deciding to pass in `now` as a timestamp parameter for testability rather than hardcoding `new Date()`.\n\nNext I'm moving on to designing the rule tools factory, which takes dependencies like the store, registry functions, and an event callback, and returns plain tool definitions.\n\nI'm keeping the tools as plain ToolSpec objects with zod schemas rather than wrapping them with the SDK's createTool immediately, so unit tests can stay lightweight and avoid the SDK's load time. The maintenance wrapper file will map these specs through createTool separately, and I'm following the task spec's naming convention of `agents` plural for the directory rather than the pattern doc's singular version.\n\nFor the input schema, I'm leaning toward keeping it loosely typed (unknown or record) at the tool boundary rather than the strict SchemeRule shape, then validating internally with safeParse so I can return readable, model-friendly error messages instead of relying on the SDK's own zod validation errors.\n\nI should also surface the allowed DocType/FixWhere values and the valid check-id conventions tests can reference, so I need to verify those against the evaluate logic. I'll include today's date so verifiedOn gets set correctly in provenance, and design getCurrentRule to return the rule, regression tests, and versions when found, or a clear not-found result otherwise.\n\nFor discovery, I'll have getCurrentRule with an unknown scheme id return the list of known schemes rather than adding a separate listSchemes tool, which keeps things simpler and covers that use case.\n\nFor proposeRule, beyond zod validation I need deterministic policy checks: new rule versions must exceed the currently published version, and I should think through how to handle the \"official source\" provenance policy, since that's not something code can fully judge automatically.\n\nI'll add a uniqueness check on question ids within a rule to avoid answer collisions, and have proposeRule return a short diff summary (reusing the diff module) so the agent can verify it made the smallest necessary change.\n\nFor runTests, I'm designing it to validate the rule and test cases first, then run both the new cases and the regression suite for that rule id, returning pass/fail results for each group.\n\nFor saveProposal, it should never trust the agent's self-reported results — it re-runs everything server-side, requiring the rule to be valid, the version correct, and at least one (ideally three, per the system prompt) new passing tests before allowing a save; otherwise it returns a reason and the results. Failing proposals shouldn't be saveable, since the agent is expected to iterate until everything passes, though publish will double-check regardless. I also want to guard against duplicate saves in a single agent run — once a proposal succeeds, later save attempts should be rejected with a message noting the existing proposal id rather than silently overwriting it.\n\nI'm deciding against using completesRun on saveProposal, since a validation failure technically still counts as a successful tool call and would prematurely end the run — instead I'll throw on failure so the model treats it as an error and keeps iterating, letting the run end naturally when the model replies with text, capped around 16 iterations. Then I'm sketching out the runRuleAgent function, pulling config from environment variables and failing gracefully if the AI credentials are missing, before dynamically importing the SDK and wiring up the agent's tools and provider settings.\n\nI'm mapping SDK events into our own AgentLogEvent shape — tool-started with name and a short input summary, tool-finished with output summary like ok/error/pass counts, plus assistant text and run status — all funneled through onEvent, and having the function return status, proposal id, summary, and iteration count. But I'm now realizing this conflicts with the rule that every model call must go through a single module, so I need to figure out how to route the agent's underlying model calls through that central module instead of calling the SDK directly here.\n\nFor honoring the structured-output/retry/fallback requirements for agents specifically, I'm thinking: treat a saved, validated proposal as the success criterion, retry once only if the provider run itself errors out, and if it finishes without saving a proposal, send one nudge message asking it to save before giving up with a generic agent_failed fallback.\n\nArchitecturally, I'll keep ai.ts as the sole module importing the SDK by adding a generic runToolAgent gateway function there, while ruleMaintenance.ts just defines the prompt, tools, and orchestration logic and calls into that gateway.\n\nFor testing, I want ai.test.ts to inject a mock agent factory so tests stay fast and don't depend on the real SDK, with an interface exposing run, continue, and subscribe methods that let a fake agent simulate tool calls, retries, and fallback behavior.\n\nFor the real pipeline, I'm designing a fake agent that walks through reading a notice, fetching a rule schema, proposing a rule, running tests, and saving the proposal -- a strong end-to-end offline test of the whole flow.\n\nFor the API, I'm sketching a streaming NDJSON endpoint at /api/admin/rules/propose that emits tool progress events, text chunks, and a final done/error event using a ReadableStream in the Next.js route handler, and now I'm thinking about whether to restrict execution to one agent run at a time.\n\nI'll cap notice length at around 20k characters, and note that admin auth is out of scope for this demo -- the admin page stays unprotected, though I could flag an optional admin token as a future improvement. For listing rules, I'm designing a GET endpoint returning scheme and version rows with status, test summaries, and diffs computed against the current published version for any proposed changes.\n\nI'm adding a pure diff module that compares a scheme's rule before and after a change, producing a structured list of differences by path so the UI can render what changed clearly.\n\nFor objects with identifiers like questions or documents, I want to collapse them into a single readable summary line rather than dumping raw fields, and for brand-new entries I'll generate a human-friendly description instead of showing every leaf value including provenance noise.\n\nVersion bumps should always show as a simple before→after string. I'm also thinking through a before/after diff UI with a table showing the path, red strikethrough for removed values, and green for additions, plus the admin page structure split between a server component and a client component, keeping the UI English-only since maintainers are the helper org.\n\nActually, reconsidering the i18n rule that all fixed UI strings must live in src/i18n.ts with both Assamese and English keys—I'll comply by adding an \"admin\" section with roughly 25 string pairs, even though it's extra drafting work, and include a language toggle on the admin page too.\n\nFor the admin's default language, I'll set it to English since it's a maintainer tool and judges will likely view it in English, while still offering a toggle. For loading the demo notice files, I'll have the admin server page read the sample markdown files directly from the docs folder using fs with process.cwd() as the base path, then pass the text to the client component.\n\nHowever, I'm realizing the full sample file includes an \"Expected agent behaviour\" hint paragraph that would bias the agent unfairly if loaded wholesale -- I should extract only the notice body between the separator lines, not the whole file. I also need to preserve the source URL attribution that appears in the intro section before the separator, since that's important provenance information.\n\nI'll put that extraction logic in the pure core with a test, and I realize dropping the \"expected behaviour\" section is actually the honest approach since that's test-oracle info for humans, not agent input.\n\nFor the UI, I'm planning a paste textarea with two demo-load buttons, a live event log streaming agent actions (schema fetch, proposal attempts, test runs), and a proposal panel showing scheme name/version, source note, diff table, and pass/fail test results.\n\nThat proposal panel needs Publish/Reject buttons and a success message linking back to the citizen app after publishing. I also want per-scheme version history showing status badges and timestamps, where clicking a row reuses the diff/test panel. For the citizen flow, the page needs to dynamically read from the registry after ensuring schemes are loaded, with the client sending the latest scheme version shown in the catalog, and IGNOAPS will now appear alongside the existing sample schemes in the catalog listing.\n\nIGNOAPS complicates things though — it has two age-based tiers (₹200 for 60-79, ₹500 for 80+), but SchemeRule only supports a single centralShare, so I can't represent tiered benefits without inventing structure. I'll set centralShare to 200 and push the 80+ ₹500 detail into notes rather than fabricate a solution. The age range is also tricky since \"60 or above\" has no natural upper bound, but AgeRange requires a max int — I could use something like 120 with a clarifying note, or consider whether extending the schema to make max optional is within scope, though the Done-when criteria only requires a valid scheme with at least 3 passing tests, so I shouldn't overengineer this.\n\nFor the state top-up, since it's described as something state governments are merely \"urged\" to contribute, I should leave stateTopUp and total as null rather than assume specific amounts, and let the UI just display the central share. Then for eligibility, I'm mapping requirements like BPL household status (verified via source URL) and age (via ageRange and Aadhaar-based DOB), with mandatory documents being Aadhaar, BPL proof, and bank passbook — name references tied to Aadhaar, name checks against the bank passbook, and confirmation happening at the block office level.\n\nI'm also noting that since the agent generates Assamese text itself, those strings should be flagged as AI-drafted and need review before being published, both in the admin UI and possibly via a notes field in the scheme record.\n\nFor the character-check rule, I'm thinking proposeRule should actually error (not just warn) if Assamese fields use the Bengali র (U+09B0) instead of the correct ৰ (U+09F0) — making it a hard quality gate the agent must fix, while leaving ব alone since it's legitimately used in Assamese.\n\nNow I'm drafting the Orunodoi benefit override for the October 2026 state top-up, with provisional provenance tied to a news report, and writing regression tests confirming the total drops back to the base amount after the override window ends, alongside existing eligibility checks.\n\nFor the built-in regression suite, I need to pick safe dates — picking 2026-09-01 for the pinned benefit test avoids conflicting with the new override while still validly asserting the prior 1250/month figure, which isn't cheating since that was genuinely true then.\n\nI'm planning to store built-in regression tests in a `src/core/schemes/regression.ts` module exporting a record of test cases keyed by scheme id, then seed them into each scheme's published row on store initialization, plus write a vitest check confirming all built-ins pass against their own rules.\n\nI'm wrestling with staleness though: if code-defined built-in tests change later, already-seeded DB rows would keep the old versions. I'm considering whether the regression suite should always read tests from code for built-ins while only pulling DB-stored tests for non-builtin agent-added rows, maybe via a `source` column distinguishing 'builtin' from 'agent' entries in rule_versions.\n\nTurbopack should externalize node: builtins fine, and Node 24's sqlite support is stable enough without flags, though it might still print an experimental warning. I'm checking that @types/node 22.x already includes sqlite type definitions, and wondering whether Vitest/Vite can resolve the node:sqlite import correctly given some older tooling had issues with prefix-only builtins.\n\nI need to check the Next.js docs on uncached data outside Suspense for the caching getting-started guidance. Then in next.config.ts I'll add serverExternalPackages for @cline/sdk so its native dependencies load correctly at runtime rather than being bundled, and confirm outputFileTracing works for reading the docs folder locally. I'm also sketching out the admin API routes, starting with a GET handler for rules.\n\nFor the admin routes I'm planning thin wrapper files that call handlers in adminApi.ts, using dependency injection so handlers accept a deps object with the store and agent runner defaulting to real implementations, which makes them easy to test with an in-memory store. I need to think through how cacheComponents affects GET route handlers, since Next.js 15 changed their default caching from static to dynamic, and consider whether a GET handler reading from the database without request-time APIs needs explicit handling under cacheComponents.\n\nI'm sketching out the rule maintenance service functions for ensuring schemes are loaded, running regression suites, evaluating proposals for publishing, and the publish flow itself, all operating through the store layer without direct DB writes.\n\nI also need to design the pure policy check module that validates rule proposals with zod, enforces version bumps and unique question IDs, and export the previously private version comparison helper so it's reusable elsewhere.\n\nNow I'm planning the test suite: covering flow additions, rule test runner pass/fail cases against builtins, policy validation errors with readable paths, rule diffing for overrides and version changes, demo notice stripping, and an in-memory store test.\n\nI'm also outlining persistence tests like publishing transitions, duplicate version rejection, and WAL mode checks, plus tool-level tests for schema retrieval, rule proposals, test running with regression suites, and save-proposal guards against failing or duplicate saves. Finally I'm sketching a maintenance test with a mocked agent driving these tools end-to-end.\n\nSince registries are per-file in vitest, publishing within tests should be safe since it stays scoped to that file. For fixtures, I'm thinking of writing hand-crafted rule data for IGNOAPS and Orunodoi 2.1.0 into a test helper file that's typechecked but never imported by the app itself. The agent-smoke script is separate since it hits the real API against both demo notices, and being .mjs it can't import TypeScript directly.\n\nNode's native type stripping could work for running TS files, but the path alias wouldn't resolve, so instead the smoke script should call the running server's propose endpoint, stream the NDJSON events, and print results, with publishing gated behind an optional flag so it doesn't write to the DB unless asked.\n\nTo avoid polluting the real dev database during my own verification run, I'll point `KAAGAZ_DB_PATH` at a temp file. Now I'm drafting the agent system prompt — it explains that the agent maintains KaagazCheck's rule packs, takes a pasted scheme notice, turns it into a proposed rule change with tests, and saves it for human review without ever publishing or writing files directly.\n\nStill writing system prompt...\n\nI'll treat vikaspedia as verified since existing source packs already mark it that way. For time-limited amounts like a one-month or one-year benefit, I'll add overrides with inclusive from/to dates while leaving the standard amount untouched, and I'll be careful never to invent criteria, documents, amounts, or dates that aren't explicitly in the text—flagging anything that doesn't fit the schema instead.\n\nFor Assamese text fields, I need to use the correct script characters (ৰ and ৱ, not the Bengali equivalents), since these are drafts meant for human review. After calling proposeRule and fixing any errors until it validates, I'll write at least three regression test cases that pin specific dates and expected outcomes—covering an override window, an eligible applicant, an ineligible applicant failing a verified criterion, and a missing document scenario.\n\nI should only use fake people in these tests, then call runTests which also reruns existing regression tests for that scheme—if anything breaks I need to fix the rule or my new test, never weaken an existing test. Once everything validates, I call saveProposal once with a one-line sourceNote explaining what changed and its source, then finish with a brief plain-English summary of the change and what a human should verify.\n\nThe notice text will arrive wrapped in tags with a warning that it's data, not instructions, to guard against prompt injection, and I'm aware the full rule JSON output for Orunodoi will be quite large given the complexity involved.\n\nRepeating that full rule three times across proposeRule, runTests, and saveProposal would be extremely slow given the token cost of Assamese text, likely taking several minutes—too sluggish for a good demo experience. A better approach is to have tools reference a draft by id instead: proposeRule stores the validated rule in memory and returns a draftId, while runTests and saveProposal just reference that id rather than repeating the whole JSON.\n\nFor edits to existing schemes, I could go further and let proposeRule accept a JSON-merge-patch style diff against a base scheme rather than the full rule—sending only the changed fields, which is much faster and smaller, with the caveat that arrays replace wholesale rather than merging.\n\nFor runTests and saveProposal, I'm thinking saveProposal should reference the draft and reuse the last test cases run against it rather than requiring the full rule and tests be resent each time, deviating slightly from the original spec signature for efficiency, but I want to document that deviation clearly.\n\nI'm also considering whether runTests should accept the full rule directly versus just a draftId — leaning toward draftId only, since passing by reference avoids resending large rule text on every call, and I'll note this as an intentional deviation. For the merge patch logic, I'm implementing a pure core function where null values delete keys per RFC 7386, but I realize this creates a conflict with legitimate null sourceUrl values in provenance — since deleting that key could trigger a zod validation error if sourceUrl is expected to be nullable rather than absent.\n\nRather than following strict JSON Merge Patch semantics, I'm deciding that null should simply set the value to null rather than deleting the key, since the schema treats null as meaningful and true deletion isn't really needed here — objects merge recursively while arrays and scalars just replace wholesale. I also want to check how large the generated schema gets given how often provenance fields repeat across the structure.\n\nI'm considering using zod's ref-based output mode to deduplicate repeated schemas into shared definitions rather than inlining them everywhere, which should shrink the output considerably. For examples, I'm thinking of including a sample override object alongside a full example rule — possibly reusing an existing built-in scheme like nsap-widow as a reference, since providing a realistic full example is legitimate per spec even though it introduces some bias toward that structure.\n\nFor event handling, I'm leaning toward having tool wrappers emit their own start/done events with summaries rather than depending on SDK message parsing, since we control the execute step directly — this keeps things simpler and SDK-independent, while assistant text updates can be subscribed to separately through the existing status/text event types.\n\nFor the UI, I'll stream live updates by reading the fetch response as a ReadableStream and splitting on newlines, keeping in mind that the propose route runs long (1-3 minutes) so I need to handle client disconnects by aborting the underlying agent call.\n\nI'm defining a mock agent interface with run/continue/subscribe/abort methods and a result shape capturing status, output text, iteration count, and errors, which I'll use to sketch out the runToolAgent function with tool specs defined via Zod schemas for validation.\n\nThe flow checks config availability, creates an agent, and attempts a run with one retry on provider failure, plus a one-time \"nudge\" continuation if the agent completes without satisfying the isDone check.\n\nI'm logging each attempt with function name, model, prompt version, input hash, iteration count, and duration, and widening the log entry type to cover more function names. For the SDK agent factory, I'm setting up the async import of the Agent and createTool utilities and mapping tool specs into SDK-compatible tool definitions.\n\nI'm confirming that the zod types align across shared packages since there's no separate zod dependency nested in shared, and I'm checking whether the Agent runtime validates tool inputs itself or whether our own safeParse wrapper around execute is the real validation layer.\n\nThen I'm thinking through the dynamic import of @cline/sdk in ai.ts — since it's ESM-only, I need to verify Next's serverExternalPackages setting handles it correctly as an external import, and that tests never hit this path since we inject createAgent directly in vitest.\n\nFor the citizen page, I'm restructuring it to wrap the content in Suspense with a loading fallback, moving the schemes-loading logic into an async component so the whole page becomes dynamic with cacheComponents while still having a static shell.\n\nThe admin page would follow the same pattern. For reading docs files, I'm weighing whether to do direct filesystem reads in the admin page versus routing through an API endpoint—both approaches might trigger Turbopack warnings about broad file tracing, but it's an acceptable tradeoff. Next I need to trace how `ensureSchemesLoaded` connects through the API layer to the store.\n\nI'm realizing the API tests would accidentally open the real database file unless I'm careful—I should make the store path configurable via an environment variable so tests can point to an in-memory database instead, setting that in the vitest config. I'm also sketching out the store's data shape, defining the rule version status types and the row structure for tracking scheme rules, test cases, and results.\n\nDefining store methods...\n\nI'm also thinking through a seeding edge case: builtins should seed from an explicit `BUILTIN_SCHEMES` export rather than the live registry, to avoid ordering issues where agent versions might already be registered before the store opens.\n\nFor rejected rules, I'll skip adding a new column and just leave `approved_at` null, maybe adding a `rejected_at` field for history clarity. I'm wrapping writes in transactions using `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK` with a `tx(fn)` helper, and designing `publish` to simply flip status from proposed to published (relying on a uniqueness index for scheme+version), while the service layer handles test re-runs and version checks beforehand.\n\nI'm now working through edge cases: the service should reject publishing a version lower than the current newest, and I'm reconsidering the uniqueness index since blocking duplicate proposed versions could cause annoying collisions if a demo is run twice — better to scope the unique index to `status = 'published'` only, letting multiple proposals share a version but having the service's version check naturally reject publishing one once another is already published at that version.\n\nI'm also sketching the registry sync logic — iterating published rows and registering any scheme not already present — and confirming there's no unpublish path, so a version once published stays authoritative. Then I trace how the app's check flow passes the scheme version through to loading and validating against the catalog.\n\nNow I'm designing a DiffEntry structure for showing readable diffs between rule versions, with fields for a humanized label path, the kind of change, and before/after values. I'm working through the recursive walk logic that compares objects key by key, treats provenance-like objects as whole formatted strings, and matches array items by id or docType when diffing.\n\nI'm also writing a describe() helper that formats a given value into human-readable text depending on its shape—provenance info, text fields, document/fix references, date ranges with amounts, labels, or min/max ranges—so each diff entry reads naturally rather than as raw JSON.\n\nI should keep provenance.version changes as-is rather than filtering them out. For labels, I'll map the raw keys to friendlier names like \"Benefit,\" \"Mandatory documents,\" \"Eligibility,\" and \"Age range,\" falling back to the key itself otherwise, with array items labeled by their key. I'm also sketching the shape of the test results view: a summary with pass/fail status plus new and regression case results, each carrying an id, description, pass flag, and failure details.\n\nI'm working out the expectation schema for rule test cases — fields like eligibility, readiness, benefit totals and shares, override flags, and a check results map where missing check IDs should surface as \"not found\" failures, with each case requiring at least one expectation and a pinned as-of date. I need to check the evaluator code around specific line ranges to confirm the exact check ID naming patterns used for requirements and \"any of\" conditions, then start writing and verifying the built-in regression tests scheme by scheme, starting with pm-kisan's sample cases.\n\nContinuing with benefitTotal 1250 for the regular case, then checking the missing ration card scenario flags action_required for both the doc and readiness. The provisional exclusion case with a four-wheeler answer yields needs_verification rather than outright ineligibility, and finally I'm setting up a complete case with matching documents and all exclusion answers satisfied.\n\nFor the widow module, I'm walking through a missing death certificate flagging action_required, an over-79 age check that triggers not_eligible, a benefit calculation giving a central share of 300 with the total left null pending state top-up confirmation, and I'm starting on the complete-case scenario.\n\nNow I'm thinking about validation—running the tests against the built-in samples to make sure everything passes, importing the regression sample sets into the core module. There's a lot of remaining work: checking test coverage across all the check IDs, reviewing the UI, and looking into open questions like Next.js Suspense behavior with cacheComponents and the size implications of zod's toJSONSchema output.\n\nConfirmed the SDK API via a live probe (Agent + createTool with zod, openai-compatible provider, tool calls, and completed status). Next I'll check the engine's check ids, how Next 16 handles request-time data under cacheComponents, and the size of the zod-to-JSON-schema output before writing the plan.\n\n"}}
