{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:12:13.842Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"text","text":"I have the context I need. Here is the plan before I write code.\n\n## Plan (T3 + the small fix)\n\n**What I found in `@cline/sdk` 0.0.92.** It only re-exports `@cline/core`, which re-exports `Agent` from `@cline/agents` and `createTool` from `@cline/shared`. Details that matter:\n- `new Agent({ providerId: \"openai-compatible\", baseUrl, apiKey, modelId, systemPrompt, tools, maxIterations })`, then `agent.run(text)` returns `{ status, outputText, iterations }`.\n- `agent.subscribe()` emits `tool-started` / `tool-finished` / `assistant-message` / `run-failed` events.\n- `createTool({ name, description, inputSchema: zod, execute })` converts zod to JSON Schema.\n- A live probe against the Cline API called a tool correctly (17+25 → 42 in 2.6 s).\n\n**0. Small fix (\"type it in\").** Add `MAIN_FIELD` per doc type, plus `isDocAdded` / `countedDocs` in `flow.ts` (written test-first). The UI shows \"details still to type\", and only counted docs go to `/api/check` and onto the cover sheet. Doc types that hold no fields (death certificate, BPL proof) count as soon as they are added.\n\n**T3 modules:**\n1. **Pure core:**\n   - `ruleTests.ts`: zod `RuleTestCase` (benefit / evaluate) and `runRuleTests`.\n   - `schemes/regression.ts`: the built-in regression suites.\n   - `ruleDiff.ts`: a readable before/after diff.\n   - `rulePolicy.ts`, enforced by code: the version must go up, and a `verified` provenance needs a URL from the notice or must be carried over unchanged.\n   - `notice.ts`: pulls the notice text out of the demo `.md` files.\n   - `agentLog.ts`: one-line summaries of each tool call.\n2. **`server/store.ts`:** the only DB writer. `node:sqlite`, WAL, transactions, a `rule_versions` table.\n3. **`server/rules.ts`:** syncs published versions into the registry (idempotent, on every request), and handles validate / test / save / publish / reject.\n4. **`server/ai.ts`:** gets `runToolAgent`, so the model calls still go through one module. It builds the `@cline/sdk` Agent, logs model / prompt version / input hash, retries once, then falls back to `{ error }`.\n5. **`server/agents/ruleMaintenance.ts`:** the 5 tools (zod inputs; tools only, no file writes) and the system prompt.\n6. **API:** a streaming NDJSON propose route, a list route, and publish / reject routes. **UI:** `/admin`. `/` becomes per-request so published rules show up at once.\n7. **Tests:** the agent is mocked; tools and store are tested directly. Plus `scripts/agent-smoke.mjs` for a live check.\n\nStarting with fix #0, test-first."}}
{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:12:13.842Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm thinking through the \"type it in\" fix: a document should count as added once its name or main field has a value, but types like death certificate or bpl_proof have no fields at all, so for those adding should probably count as present immediately.\n\nFor docs with a defined main field, I need a helper that maps each doc type to its relevant field (name for most, ownerNames for land records, null for ones without fields), then checks if that field is filled before treating the document as counted. The tricky part is deciding where this logic should live—applying it inside the core evaluation engine risks breaking cases where an AI-read photo has an unreadable name but the document is still genuinely present, so the safer approach is to implement this as a separate helper used by the client/flow layer rather than altering the engine itself.\n\nI'm leaning toward filtering documents at the app level before sending them to the check endpoint, while also updating the DocsStep UI to show a \"waiting for details\" state for documents that exist but aren't filled in yet, alongside a remove option. The ConfirmStep still needs to display these blank documents so users can type in the missing info, and I should route the same filtered \"counted\" document list to both the check call and the cover sheet for consistency.\n\nI need to add a new i18n key for this pending state in both English and Assamese, making sure the Assamese translation avoids using the Bengali-style \"র\" character. Then shifting focus to the T3 database design, I'm weighing whether node:sqlite will work cleanly with Next's Turbopack bundling and whether Vitest can resolve that same node builtin without issues.\n\nDeciding the DB should live at `data/kaagaz.db`, configurable via an environment variable with a sensible default, while tests use an in-memory database instead. I'm starting to sketch the store module that will act as the sole DB writer, beginning with its type definitions.\n\nDefining the store's interface...\n\nI'm also sketching the SQLite schema for rule_versions with a partial unique index on (scheme_id, version) excluding rejected rows, so two non-rejected proposals can't share a version—though I need to think through what happens if an agent tries proposing a version that's already been proposed.\n\nFor resolving collisions, I'm deciding that saveProposal should mark an existing proposed row as \"superseded\"/rejected if a new proposal comes in for the same scheme+version, while erroring if that version is already published. I'll also need to enforce that new versions must exceed the current published version using a compareVersions helper, and figure out how the registry syncs published scheme versions on startup and publish, treating built-ins as version 1.\n\nThis gets called lazily at the start of every request that reads schemes, but since Next might not share module instances across route bundles, I can't rely on in-memory state alone — better to make syncRegistry idempotent and re-check the DB's published rows on every request since that's a cheap single SELECT.\n\nThat ensures published versions always take effect immediately in the citizen flow, and since getScheme sorts by version, a published version will always win over the built-in. The home page needs to read fresh DB state at request time rather than being statically prerendered, so I'm wrapping it in Suspense with an async server component that calls connection() first before querying.\n\nI also need to make sure the /api/check route syncs published rules before calling getScheme, though I should watch out since that sync touches the actual database file, which could affect test runs that import api.ts directly.\n\nI'm solving this by using an environment variable for the DB path, setting it to in-memory within the vitest config so tests stay isolated. The store should be a singleton opened from store.ts only, keeping all DB access centralized there while rules.ts handles the service logic through the store and registry.\n\nFor the regression tests model in ruleTests.ts, I'm defining a schema for rule test cases covering benefit and evaluate kinds, with fields for date and expected results like total and central share values.\n\nStill writing schema...\n\nI'm adding a validation refinement requiring the expect object to have at least one key, then sketching out a runRuleTests function that executes cases and labels each result as new or regression, tracking pass/fail status.\n\nI also need baseline regression suites for the three built-in schemes stored as exported test case data, and I'm now checking whether the Orunodoi October override breaks an existing regression test that expected a different benefit amount on that date.\n\nRather than guess outcomes, I should actually run the regression suite to verify—tests accumulate as built-in cases plus all published DB versions for that scheme, and any proposed version must pass all of them. For IGNOAPS, since it's new, there are no existing tests to satisfy, only new cases to define. I also need to locate the agent tools file, likely at src/server/agents/ruleMaintenance.ts per the backlog, even though systemPatterns suggests a different path.\n\nFor the getRuleSchema tool, I'm working out its return shape: a JSON schema generated from SchemeRule via zod's toJSONSchema in input mode, plus test case schema, vocabulary terms, a compact example rule, and conventions. I'm checking how zod 4 handles refinements (ignored, falling back to base types) and custom types like IsoDate or URL fields, and confirming that input mode correctly treats defaulted fields as optional.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the remaining tools: getCurrentRule to fetch an existing rule plus its regression tests and version history or a list of scheme IDs if not found; proposeRule to validate a rule against the schema and policy checks like version increments and provisional-vs-verified requirements, returning either a diff summary or validation errors; runTests to validate and execute both new and regression test cases against a rule; and saveProposal to persist a validated proposal along with its tests and a source note.\n\nI'm deciding that code should always independently re-run tests rather than trust the agent's claims, and save proposals as \"proposed\" with computed results. I'll refuse to save if tests fail or there are no new tests, keeping the flow strict rather than letting failing proposals linger -- and I'll make Publish re-run tests against the current regression set at publish time too, since the regression set may have changed since the proposal was saved.\n\nFor the tool's `rule` input schema, I want to avoid forcing the full SchemeRule zod schema into the tool's input (too strict, opaque SDK errors) and instead use a loose object or record type at the tool boundary, then validate with SchemeRule internally to produce readable error messages back to the agent.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the actual agent execution flow: `runRuleAgent` takes the notice plus event/dependency hooks, builds an Agent bound to a store/context, and returns a status with proposal ID, summary, and event log. I'm sketching the agent config — provider, model, system prompt, tool bindings, iteration cap around 16, and some abort timeout around 180 seconds — while keeping in mind the rule that all AI calls must route through a single module.\n\nTo honor that, I want `ai.ts` to expose something like `createClineAgent` that builds the Agent from env config and logs model, prompt version, and input hash. For structured output, the tool-call results already get zod validation, so the retry policy becomes: if a run fails or completes without a saved proposal, retry once with a fresh agent, then fall back to an error object. I'm planning a generic `runToolAgent` helper in ai.ts to implement this.\n\nWriting the agent retry function...\n\nI'm weighing whether retrying doubles total latency, deciding an overall timeout budget keeps it acceptable, and starting to think about how the propose endpoint should stream tool-call events back to the client.\n\nThe streaming response needs to emit events for tool calls, text, completion, and errors, but since full rule JSON payloads are too large to send raw, I'm designing summary objects instead—capturing rule id/version and counts for tool inputs, and error/pass-fail counts for outputs. I'm considering where to place a pure summarization helper that condenses tool name, arguments, and results into a readable log line, weighing whether it belongs in core or server-specific code since it needs tool-name awareness.\n\nFor the diff logic, I'm designing a pure function that compares two rule versions path-by-path and emits structured changes (added/removed/changed) with compact readable before/after values, using id-based keying for arrays like questions or notes where it makes sense, and falling back to index-based diffing otherwise, plus truncating large object dumps for readability.\n\nFor a new scheme where the before side is null, I'll just emit top-level entries per key with compact formatted values, which keeps things readable. Now I'm sketching the admin API: a propose endpoint streaming NDJSON from a notice, and a rules listing endpoint returning schemes with their current and built-in versions.\n\nI'm also designing how diffs get computed on read against a base version rather than stored, since built-ins live in the registry and published rules stay synced, and a publish endpoint that reruns regression tests, enforces version ordering, and returns 409 if tests fail or the rule wasn't proposed first.\n\nFor auth I'm skipping real security for the hackathon and just noting it as an open issue, maybe flagging an optional admin token later. I also need to be careful that GET route handlers reading synchronous DB data could get prerendered at build time under cacheComponents, so I have to call connection() inside the route file itself rather than in a shared admin module, since importing next/server there would break under vitest.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the admin UI structure—putting it under src/app/admin with a client component, and deciding the admin tool can be English-only since it's for rule maintainers rather than citizens, unlike the Assamese-default app. I'm weighing whether to fold admin strings into the shared i18n file (which requires matching as/en keys) versus keeping them local to the component and just flagging the inconsistency in my log, leaning toward the latter with a header like \"Rule...\" as the first admin page element.\n\nI'm planning the notice loader section with demo buttons for sample notices, and deciding to read the actual markdown docs at runtime via fs rather than duplicating their content into a separate source file, since the server can read from the project root at request time—wrapping this in a dynamic/Suspense boundary since it needs live file access.\n\nSince the full docs include a paragraph revealing the expected agent behavior, which feels like cheating if passed into the agent, I should extract just the notice text between separator lines rather than the whole file, while still preserving the source URL for provenance.\n\nI'll write a function to extract the notice body between the first two dividers, prepending \"Source: url\" when found in the preamble, and verify it works for both IGNOAPS and orunodoi cases. The load buttons should populate the editable textarea, and I also want to design a live agent log showing streamed tool calls like schema lookups, rule proposals, test runs, and saves.\n\nEach proposal card should display the scheme name, version change, source note, a diff table, test results with pass/fail badges, and Publish/Reject buttons—where Publish stays disabled if any test fails. I'm also planning version history per scheme (built-in plus DB entries with status), a refresh after each run via the rules API, and confirming that new schemes like IGNOAPS flow correctly into the citizen-facing catalog. Now I need to draft the agent system prompt for rule maintenance.\n\nI'll enforce this in checkProposalPolicy: any provenance marked \"verified\" must have its sourceUrl appear verbatim in the notice text, otherwise it's an error, and new version numbers must exceed the current one. For change proposals the id must already exist, while new scheme proposals require a fresh id with a suggested starting version of 1.0.0. As for whether IGNOAPS should be marked verified using the Vikaspedia URL, I'll leave that judgment to the agent since the policy permits it as long as the URL is present in the notice.\n\nFor Orunodoi, since there's no official notice URL, the policy forces the provenance to remain provisional. I also need at least three tests for IGNOAPS covering cases like an eligible 65-year-old BPL applicant, a 55-year-old who doesn't qualify, and the monthly benefit amount — though I realize IGNOAPS actually has age-tiered benefits (₹200 for ages 60-79, ₹500 for 80+), which the current Benefit model doesn't support, so I'll need to represent that limitation via a note rather than inventing new criteria.\n\nFor stateTopUp, since the text only says states are \"urged to contribute\" without confirmation, I should set it to null, meaning the total benefit calculation falls back to just the central share of 200. For AgeRange, since the policy says \"60 years or above\" without an upper bound, I need a convention for open-ended ranges — using a cap like 120 with an explanatory note seems like the reasonable approach, and I should document this convention explicitly in the schema output so the agent knows how to handle \"X or above\" cases, along with documenting the test case schema and an example test requiring proper ExtractedDoc fields like aadhaar and dob.\n\nNow I'm mapping document types — BPL proof, Aadhaar card for age/dob verification, and bank passbook for name checks — then working out the Orunodoi benefit override scenario where a temporary state top-up of 1500 applies for October 2026 before reverting to the base 1250, requiring test cases for both the override period and the regression back to baseline. I also need to flag that AI-generated Assamese translations should be labeled as drafts in the admin diff view, since they haven't been verified by a native speaker.\n\nI'm also considering guarding against the agent mistakenly using Bengali script characters (like ব০৯বর০) instead of proper Assamese orthography — rather than hard-failing validation, I'll make this a warning so the model isn't forced into a fix loop it might struggle with.\n\nFor testing, I plan to mock the agent in runToolAgent within ai.ts, injecting a fake Agent factory whose run() method directly invokes tools to simulate tool calls and emits the corresponding events.\n\nI should also verify retry behavior when the first attempt doesn't finish, confirm the fallback error path triggers when there's no config, and ensure logs capture model, promptVersion, and inputHash without leaking notice text.\n\nFor ruleMaintenance, I want to test runRuleMaintenance with a scripted fake agent that calls the real tools in sequence—getCurrentRule, modify the rule, runTests, saveProposal—for the Orunodoi notice, then confirm the proposal is stored and tests pass; I'll do the same scripted flow for IGNOAPS. This serves as offline proof that fixtures pass tests, publishing makes IGNOAPS selectable in the catalog, and Orunodoi values resolve correctly (Oct 1500/Nov 1250), with fixtures defined directly in the test file.\n\nFor the live smoke script, I realize .mjs can't import TypeScript with path aliases directly even with Node's native type stripping, so instead I'll have the script hit the running server's API endpoints over HTTP—posting both demo notices extracted from docs, printing the tool log, then fetching the rules list to show proposal test results, with publishing gated behind a flag. I plan to run this myself against a production server start for live verification.\n\nTo keep the real database clean, I'll point the smoke run at a temporary DB path via an env variable rather than the default data file, and I'll set the Vitest config to use an in-memory DB path so unit tests never touch disk at all. Now I'm thinking through build concerns, starting with whether Turbopack can bundle the Cline SDK properly.\n\nSince @cline/sdk pulls in the whole @cline/core stack with heavy dependencies like protobufjs and dynamic requires, I need to mark those packages as external in the Next.js config. I'll stick with importing Agent and createTool from @cline/sdk per the documented API, but lazy-load it via a dynamic import inside the agent run function so the page itself stays lightweight and tests don't trigger that heavy load.\n\nFor testing, I'll exercise the plain tool handler functions directly in unit tests, with one dedicated test that actually builds the tools through the real createTool to confirm names and schemas line up.\n\nNow I'm also thinking about node:sqlite under Turbopack -- that likely needs the same external treatment.\n\nOn placement: since every model call must flow through ai.ts, I'll add `runToolAgent` there to handle the dynamic sdk import and Agent construction, while keeping tool specs (name, description, zod schema, execute) as plain objects in ruleTools.ts that ai.ts wraps with createTool.\n\nFor testing, I can inject a fake `loadSdk` dependency returning stub Agent/createTool implementations. Then ai.ts subscribes to agent events and forwards simplified ones for tool-started, tool-finished (mapped by toolCallId), text output, and attempt count, with 20 max iterations and a 150s timeout per attempt, plus an `isDone()` callback supplied by the caller to decide on retries.\n\nI also need to widen the AiLogEntry type's fn field to accept \"runToolAgent\" alongside \"extractDocument\", with outcome values like ok/incomplete/failed/unavailable/error plus iteration counts and timing. For the store, I'm planning a `tx(fn)` transaction helper using BEGIN IMMEDIATE/COMMIT/ROLLBACK with node:sqlite, enabling WAL mode except for in-memory databases.\n\nThen I'm sketching the rule maintenance service in `src/server/rules.ts`: functions to sync published rules into the registry idempotently, fetch the current rule for a scheme, gather regression tests, validate a proposed rule against schema and policy, run tests on a proposal, and save an accepted proposal.\n\nI also need lifecycle operations—publishing, rejecting, and listing rule versions with diffs—plus the agent tools in `ruleMaintenance.ts` and admin API handlers that call into this service.\n\nFor the policy checker itself, I'm designing a pure function that enforces version ordering, prevents id collisions for new rules, and requires that any verified provenance either appears in the notice text or matches an unchanged provenance from the current rule.\n\nI refine this: a verified provenance should be allowed if it's deep-equal to an existing verified provenance from the current version, or if its source URL is referenced in the notice—otherwise an agent could sneak a provisional claim past verification by reusing an official URL without citing it. I'll write a generic recursive walker to collect all \"provenance\" objects from the rule structure, and add a warning check for stray Bengali characters inside any field ending in \"As\".\n\nFor verifiedOn I want it set to today's date, so I'll pass `today` into the system prompt.\n\nFor the regression baseline, I'm planning one documents set covering PM-KISAN cases A and B plus the widow case C and Orunodoi, with expected outcomes drawn from the existing tests: case A likely eligible/ready for review, case B requiring action, case C requiring action due to missing death certificate. I'm noting expected benefit values—PM-KISAN's annual amount, the widow pension's shares, and Orunodoi's total—and need to check the evaluate test fixtures for the Orunodoi case specifics.\n\nSince the regression date is fixed and Orunodoi's evaluate cases only check eligibility and readiness rather than exact benefit values, the October override shouldn't cause issues. Given the scope of this work, I'll need to write files in chunks to stay under the editor's character limit, and I'm sketching out the shape of the proposal row view for the UI along with how built-in schemes should appear in the version history.\n\nFor the admin page, I'm planning a server component that loads initial data via the same list function plus demo notices, with a client component handling actions and refetching. I want streaming events and shared admin view types to live in pure core modules so the client only imports types, and I'm defining a `summarizeTool` helper for turning tool calls into readable log strings. Now I'm starting to define the test-case schema, beginning with a validator for case IDs...\n\nDefining more test case schemas...\n\nDefining the test case and run result shapes, where regression tests accumulate across published versions while new tests stay scoped to the current one. For benefit checks I'm comparing each expected key strictly against the computed result, and for evaluate checks I'm matching eligibility, readiness, and individual check ids against the report, flagging any missing checks by id. I also want a zod schema to validate the stored test results JSON.\n\nI'm checking that re-parsing an already-normalized rule output through the schema stays valid since defaults get filled in consistently, so stored rule JSON is safe to validate on read. The catalog and scheme selection logic look fine as-is, and since the registry retains old versions, a check running against a stale catalog version still resolves correctly even after a newer version gets published — I just need to make sure `handleCheck` syncs published rules before fetching the scheme.\n\nSince vitest workers run test files in parallel with separate module instances, each `:memory:` store and registry stays isolated per file, so publishing a new rule version in one test file won't leak into another. I'll use `createStore(\":memory:\")` directly in store tests and rely on a `globalThis` singleton for the real store to survive Next.js dev HMR. I also need to double check the `has()` semantics in the DocsStep fix.\n\nNow I'm ready to start implementing: fixing the docFields/flow logic and DocsStep tests first, then building out ruleTests, regression schemes, ruleDiff, rulePolicy, notice extraction, and agentLog with their tests, followed by the store, rules, AI tool-agent runner, and finally the ruleMaintenance agent with mocked-agent tests.\n\nAfter that I'll wire up the admin API routes and UI page, update config files like next.config and .gitignore, add the smoke-test script, and finish with docs/memory updates and a commit. Before diving in, I want to quickly check the evaluate.test fixtures for the Orunodoi and PM-Kisan scheme cases so I can verify my regression expectations against the existing Orunodoi test logic.\n\n"}}
