{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:16:44.145Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"With that fix done, I'm moving to T3 and designing the rule test case structure, planning a discriminated union schema that covers both \"benefit\" test cases checking calculation totals and shares, and \"evaluate\" test cases that validate eligibility logic against documents and answers.\n\nI'm thinking through requiring optional documents defaulting to empty arrays, and writing a runRuleTests function that pairs each case with evaluate and benefitOn to produce pass/fail results, with a validation refinement ensuring every case has at least one real expectation to prevent meaningless tests.\n\nNow I'm working out the built-in regression suites for schemes like pm-kisan, orunodoi-3, and nsap-widow, trying to reconcile how \"built-in schemes are version 1\" should translate into seeding behavior in the rule_versions store on startup and publish.\n\nI'm deciding to keep the existing semver (2.0.0) for built-ins since tests depend on it, treating them instead as the baseline entry in a scheme's published history, and I'm leaning toward having each scheme's regression suite accumulate the built-in tests plus tests from every subsequently published version so nothing gets lost as schemes evolve.\n\nI need to be careful that built-in regression tests don't pin benefit amounts on dates affected by the October override, since that would conflict with the new override logic — so I'll write those regression dates outside October 2026 to keep the old tests valid while using October AS_OF dates only for eligibility/readiness checks.\n\nI'm cataloguing the expected built-in regression outcomes across pm-kisan, orunodoi-3, and nsap-widow samples to make sure my implementation still produces the correct eligible/action_required statuses and benefit totals.\n\nI should make sure the check-id naming convention (doc_, name_, field_, age, req_, anyof, excl_, ext_) is clearly documented in getRuleSchema so agents writing new scheme tests know how to reference checks. Also need to flag that IGNOAPS only supports a single flat benefit amount, not age-tiered rates, so I shouldn't fabricate an age-based tier that the schema doesn't support.\n\nExtending the schema for age-banded benefits is scope creep beyond the task requirements, so I'll keep it as-is and have the agent note the 80+ amount separately rather than modeling it structurally. I still need to verify whether these notes actually surface in the report view, and I'm also noting the state top-up language as a related detail to address.\n\nFor provenance, I'm deciding that \"verified\" status should require the sourceUrl to literally appear in the notice text or match the currently published rule — enforced in code rather than left to the model's judgment, since a government portal source isn't automatically \"official\" enough. I'll also make sure the prompt supplies today's date so the agent can set verifiedOn correctly.\n\nFor versioning policy: new schemes just need any valid semver, but changes to existing schemes require a version strictly greater than the latest published one, and I want to allow rejected proposals to reuse a version number while blocking duplicates among proposed or published rows. The tricky edge case is two pending proposals for the same scheme — publishing one could invalidate the other's version ordering, so the publish step needs to re-check version validity at that point.\n\nFor the store module, I'm settling on node:sqlite with a configurable database path, keeping only this module touching the database, and wrapping writes in explicit transactions while letting reads happen outside of them for simplicity. Publishing will also re-run the regression suite as a safety check, returning a 409 if anything fails after human approval.\n\nNow I'm designing the rule_versions table schema, with columns tracking the scheme id, version, rule and test JSON payloads, test results, a status enum restricted to proposed/published/rejected, source notes, and timestamps for creation and approval.\n\nI'm also adding a partial unique index so only non-rejected versions enforce uniqueness per scheme/version pair, plus a baseVersion column to track what a proposal was diffed against for history purposes. Now I'm sketching out the store API, starting with the openStore function and its insertProposal method signature.\n\nStill writing store methods...\n\nI'm deciding that validation should happen at the rules.ts layer rather than in the store itself, so the store returns raw JSON and the registry is responsible for parsing it with zod schemas.\n\nRegarding node:sqlite, I'm thinking through whether Next's build will correctly externalize this Node builtin given its experimental status, and whether cacheComponents affects how the admin routes interact with it.\n\nFor the admin page, I'll make it a client component that fetches the rules list from the API on mount, keeping the shell static and avoiding prerender complications. For the citizen-facing `/` route, since the catalog needs to reflect newly published schemes, I'm planning to wrap the dynamic part in an async component using `connection()` under Suspense so it can re-sync and rebuild the catalog per request while the rest of the page stays prerendered.\n\nI also realize `/api/check` needs to call `syncPublishedRules()` before fetching a scheme, since I can't guarantee the in-memory registry is shared consistently across route bundles in dev or production builds — making that sync call idempotent is the safest approach.\n\nThe sync logic itself should read published rows from the DB and register any that aren't already present by id+version, which is cheap since the table is small. Since publishing enforces that a new version must exceed the current one, there's no conflict risk, and `getScheme` naturally returns the highest semver as the latest published version, keeping version history and catalog listings consistent.\n\nA proposed-but-unpublished version must never get registered — runTests works directly off the candidate rule object rather than the registry, which keeps this safe. Now I'm thinking through the agent tools module, where a session context holds notice, date, store/registry dependencies, and accumulating state like last validation, last test run, and saved proposal id. I'm sketching the first tool, which exposes the rule schema, vocabulary (doc types, fix locations), check id documentation, and test case schema as JSON schema outputs.\n\nStill designing the schema output... I want to include a compact example scheme like nsap-widow as the smallest full illustration, plus a list of existing schemes with their ids, versions, and names so the agent can decide whether to create new or modify. Then I'm defining a second tool, getCurrentRule, which looks up a scheme by id and returns either the found rule with its regression tests or a not-found response listing existing schemes instead.\n\nFor the third tool, proposeRule, I'm weighing whether the input schema should accept the rule as an arbitrary JSON record or be constrained to the full SchemeRule JSON schema — the latter keeps the model on-schema but I need to check how that interacts with strict mode in OpenAI-compatible providers. The tool would validate via SchemeRule's safeParse plus policy checks like version bumps and verified URLs in the notice, returning either a success result with diff info or a structured list of validation errors.\n\nFor the fourth tool, runTests, it takes a rule and test cases, validates both, then runs the new cases alongside a regression suite of built-in and previously published tests for that scheme.\n\nFor saveProposal, I want the server to re-validate everything and actually re-run the tests itself rather than trust the model's claim of success. I'll enforce a minimum of three new tests for any proposal, whether it's a new scheme or a modification, then insert the proposal into the store with a \"proposed\" status and attached test results.\n\nI'm thinking about session semantics: should a session allow only one save? I'll allow multiple attempts but reject further saves once one succeeds, pointing back to the existing proposal id. I also want a successful save to end the agent run via lifecycle completion, but that means I need to be careful — any non-throwing return would count as \"successful\" and prematurely end the loop. So validation failures should throw an error instead of returning a normal result, keeping the run alive until an actual valid save happens.\n\nFor sourceNote, I'll keep it simple: the agent supplies a short summary string and the server just stores it as-is without composing anything extra.\n\nNow I'm designing the pure policy checker: it compares the proposed rule's version against the current one to ensure it's strictly greater, and if there's no current rule it just passes since it's a new scheme. I also need to walk the rule recursively to find every provenance entry, confirming that any marked \"verified\" either has its sourceUrl present in the notice text or exactly matches an existing provenance entry in the current rule, while provisional entries with no sourceUrl are allowed through.\n\nSince compareVersions currently lives unexported in schemes/index.ts, I'm planning to pull it out into a new core/semver.ts module so I can reuse it, then turning to a diff utility that flattens before/after rule trees into leaf paths and classifies each difference as added, removed, or changed.\n\nFor brand-new schemes where there's no \"before\" to compare, dumping every leaf as \"added\" would be overwhelming, so instead I'm designing a summarizeRule function that produces a readable overview -- name, benefit, age range, docs, requirements, exclusions, provenance -- and reserving the full diff view for actual changes, collapsing provenance objects into compact version strings.\n\nShould I show the version field in the diff too? I'll include it since knowing the version bump is useful context.\n\nFor keying arrays during diffing, I'll match objects by id or docType when present, falling back to index otherwise. Now I'm moving on to how notice extraction works, looking at how demo files structure their header, delimiters, and expected behavior sections.\n\nI'm weighing whether the \"Expected agent behaviour\" hint should be fed to the agent or kept separate as grading instructions, and noticing the header lines differ between demos—IGNOAPS includes a source URL while Orunodoi includes a provisional caveat instead. For the \"load demo notice\" buttons, I'm leaning toward loading the file with the instructional section stripped out but keeping the header intact, since it carries useful context like the source URL or caveat.\n\nSince readFileSync is synchronous, it'll complete during prerendering and get baked into the static shell at build time, which works well for loading the demo notices from the docs folder. I'll put that read directly in the admin page server component, then move on to looking at the runToolAgent function in ai.ts.\n\nFor the agent, I'm treating validated tool inputs as the structured output rule requires, so I'll define a request interface for the tool agent with a prompt version, system prompt, user message, tool list, max iterations, a done-check function, and optional event callback and abort signal.\n\nI'm defining the result type and the runToolAgent function with injected dependencies for env, logging, and agent creation. The flow checks config availability first, computes an input hash from the prompt, then tries one attempt creating an agent and subscribing to events—if the done check passes it's a success, otherwise it retries once with a nudge message about the failed proposal before falling back to an error, logging each step under the runToolAgent function name.\n\nNow I'm worried about where to import the @cline/sdk package since ai.ts is also used by the extract and check API routes, making that a heavy static import for every request. I'm thinking dynamic imports inside runToolAgent are better, and considering whether to mark these SDK packages as external server packages so Next.js requires them at runtime instead of bundling them, since they're ESM-only and might not bundle cleanly with Turbopack.\n\nDespite being tempted to use the lighter `@cline/agents` package directly, the task specifies `@cline/sdk`'s Agent per stack rules, so I'll stick with that import and configure externals accordingly, while confirming the static createTool import in ruleMaintenance.ts should still work fine in Vitest.\n\nI should double-check that createTool's zod schema conversion is compatible with our resolved zod version, since the probe output looked correct. I also need to plan the agent's likely tool-call budget — schema lookup, rule proposal, and test execution — when setting maxIterations.\n\nI'm weighing timeout concerns since a full run generating large JSON rules could take a couple minutes, which should be fine for streaming. The bigger issue is token cost: re-sending the full rule object across proposeRule, runTests, and saveProposal triples the payload, so I'm considering having tools reference \"the last proposed rule\" instead of re-emitting it each time to cut down on redundant tokens.\n\nRe-emitting the whole rule risks mangling the Assamese text, so I'm weighing a patch-based alternative where proposeRule takes a base scheme plus a set of path-value changes instead of the full rule body. That feels more robust and better fits the \"smallest change\" principle, so I could support both full `rule` for new schemes and a `baseSchemeId` + `set` map for edits.\n\nI'm deciding runTests and saveProposal should default to operating on the session's last proposed rule, keeping their rule/tests parameters optional for compatibility with the spec's required-argument version. Now I'm sketching the session state structure to track this.\n\nI'm separating concerns: the service layer in server/rules.ts handles syncing published rules, regression testing, validation, running proposal tests, saving/publishing/rejecting proposals, while the agent file wires these into tools.\n\nI'm settling on the agents/ directory path per the task instructions, and sketching the propose API route that streams NDJSON events for tool and text updates during rule maintenance.\n\nFor the GET /api/admin/rules route, I'm realizing that since cacheComponents could prerender the route at build time, I need to explicitly call connection() before querying the database to avoid serving stale build-time data. I'm also planning the publish and related endpoints for rule versions.\n\nI'll skip auth for admin routes since it's a hackathon demo, just noting it as an open issue rather than implementing a token system. I'm now sketching the RuleVersionView shape that the UI will consume—covering scheme metadata, diffs, test results, and regression results—plus how testResultJson stores pass/fail outcomes and timestamps.\n\nFor the admin page itself, I'm planning a server component that reads demo notices and passes them to a client AdminApp component, which will have a textarea, demo buttons, a run button, a live log stream, and a result card showing diffs, test outcomes, and publish/reject actions, along with a history section grouped by scheme showing version status badges.\n\nOn language, I'm weighing whether the admin UI needs both Assamese and English per the i18n rule, but since this is a maintainer tool rather than citizen-facing, I'm leaning toward English-only for admin strings and flagging that decision rather than doubling translation work.\n\nFor publish propagation, pages stay dynamic and `/api/check` syncs published rules before reading the scheme, with mid-flow users keeping whatever version they started with since schemeVersion is passed along. I'm handling the \"load latest published version on startup and publish\" requirement by having `syncPublishedRules()` called lazily by every reader (page, check, admin routes) plus directly on publish, rather than needing a dedicated startup hook.\n\nFor resilience, if the store fails to open—like on a read-only filesystem—the sync should catch and log the error rather than crash the citizen flow, falling back to built-ins.\n\nFor testing, I'm planning offline test suites: one for running rule tests against built-in schemes checking benefit/evaluate expectations and schema validation, one for regression testing that built-in rule suites pass (including checking an October override for orunodoi still passes regression with correct October/November benefit amounts), and one for rule diffing.\n\nI'm also covering version bumps and notice verification, demo notice extraction, an in-memory store test (insert, list, publish with transaction/status checks, reject, unique constraints, ordering, approval timestamps), and a service/tools test using the memory store to verify the proposeRule tool against an IGNOAPS fixture, running tests, and saving proposals.\n\nBeyond that, I'm checking that publish updates the registry scheme and catalog, that changes apply via `change.set`, that policy failures are caught, and that saveProposal rejects failing or under-tested proposals. I'm also covering the rule maintenance agent test using a mocked agent to simulate tool call sequences and verify event streaming, retries, and fallback errors, plus the runToolAgent tests for missing API keys, single retries, and hashed logging, along with API route tests for propose, list, publish, and reject using an injected in-memory store.\n\nFor store injection, I'm considering having `getStore()` read a `KAAGAZ_DB_PATH` env var (defaulting to a file path) and exporting a `useStore(store)` setter so tests can swap in an in-memory store before each run. Since vitest isolates module graphs per file, registry mutations from publishing persist across tests within the same file, so I need to design test ordering with that in mind, and I'm also planning a fixture file holding an example \"good\" rule proposal for reuse across agent tests.\n\nI'm placing the fixtures file at `src/server/agents/__fixtures__/proposals.ts` since it's just a non-test module imported by tests, not shipped in production. Now I'm thinking through the agent smoke script, which needs to run both demo notices against the real API — but since it's a plain `.mjs` file, it can't import TypeScript source directly without a build step first.\n\nSimplest approach is to hit a running server's propose endpoint via HTTP, so `agent-smoke.mjs` just takes a base URL and prints streamed logs and results. I'll add an optional `--publish` flag so it can also verify the proposal becomes selectable afterward, by checking the admin rules list or the home page HTML for the scheme name.\n\nSince the done criteria calls for a live check, I should actually run this against a real server with fresh API credits. To keep the demo DB clean I'll point KAAGAZ_DB_PATH at a temporary file for the smoke run, then publish within that temp DB to confirm everything works end-to-end.\n\nLooking at the system prompt for the Rule Maintenance Agent, it's clear the agent reads a notice, checks the schema and current rule for the relevant scheme, decides whether this is a new scheme or a version bump on an existing one, and proposes the smallest possible change — never publishing or writing files directly, only working through tools.\n\nEach addition or change must carry provenance: the notice title, the relevant clause, an effective date if given, today's verification date, and the new version number, with status marked \"verified\" only if an official source URL exists, otherwise \"provisional.\" The agent must never fabricate criteria, amounts, documents, or dates — anything ambiguous gets logged as a provisional scheme note instead.\n\nTime-bounded changes should use benefit.overrides with inclusive date ranges while preserving the regular amount, and I need to write at least three regression tests covering dates inside/outside any window plus eligibility edge cases like age limits or missing documents. The runTests step must pass both new and existing regression tests without ever weakening the existing ones, then I save the proposal with a one-sentence source note and close with a two-line summary — all required Assamese fields (nameAs, labelAs, textAs, noteAs) need simple, correct Assamese using ৰ and ৱ.\n\nI'm also noting that since a human reviewer checks the Assamese later, draft-quality translations are acceptable as long as they're flagged as draft in the source note. For verification status, since the notice references a vikaspedia URL (a government-run portal), marking the scheme as \"verified\" is reasonable and consistent with how other schemes like nsap-widow use similar official sources. I'm sketching out an example evaluate-type test case that includes an Aadhaar document with name and date-of-birth fields to test age-based eligibility rules.\n\nNow I'm defining how runRuleTests should report results — comparing eligibility, readiness, and check outcomes for evaluate-type tests, and comparing numeric benefit fields like total, centralShare, and stateTopUp for benefit-type tests, with errors caught and reported as failures. I'm starting to write the zod schema for RuleTestCase's benefit expectation object.\n\nDefining the benefit and evaluate test case variants...\n\nNow I'm thinking about test id formatting constraints and uniqueness rules — ids should match a lowercase alphanumeric pattern and stay unique within a list, with new and regression suites tracked separately via a \"new\"/\"regression\" tag so collisions between them aren't an issue. I'm also working out how the regression suite for a scheme combines the built-in code-based suite with tests from every published version of that scheme in the store, in publish order, so a newly added scheme starts empty until its first publish.\n\nA tricky issue surfaces though: if a later version permanently changes a base amount (say 1250 to 1500), older regression tests tied to that amount would break, since rules don't track dated historical base amounts — this is a real limitation I should flag as an open issue rather than try to solve now, since the system prompt insists regression tests shouldn't be weakened and leaves rejection to a human reviewer.\n\nMoving on, I verify the semver comparison lives in core/semver.ts, and check that the benefit override provenance setup satisfies the schema requirements for status, source, and bilingual notes. I want to confirm the override actually surfaces in the report through the benefit card component, and verify that database access stays lazy so nothing opens a connection at build time — the admin route and root page both defer via the connection() pattern, which is the right approach.\n\nI'm now checking whether node:sqlite has proper TypeScript definitions in the installed @types/node version, and whether Turbopack correctly recognizes it as an external Node builtin rather than trying to bundle it — if that fails, falling back to a JSON file store is an option. I'll also note that Node 24 may have changed the experimental warning behavior for sqlite, though that's a minor concern.\n\nFor the database setup, I'm enabling WAL journal mode (which is a no-op for in-memory databases), wrapping writes in explicit transactions with BEGIN IMMEDIATE/COMMIT and rollback on error, and creating the data directory recursively unless the path is \":memory:\". For logging, I want structured JSON output that includes tool counts and outcomes but never the actual notice text — only its hash, since the notice itself is already public. I'm also confirming that maxIterations and toolExecution settings exist on the agent runtime config as expected.\n\nI'm setting tool execution to sequential to keep session state ordering deterministic, wiring up abort-on-disconnect by listening for the request signal and calling agent.abort(), and adding a default 240-second timeout per attempt. I'm also figuring out how agent events like tool-started and tool-finished get converted into our NDJSON event format, with pure summarizer functions handling the tool call and result summaries separately in the agent log module.\n\nRather than generating summaries generically from raw outputs, I think it's simpler to have tools emit their own log entries directly into the session with exact summary text, while the route streams those alongside status and tool-phase events.\n\nFor tool-finished events, I can extract the summary from the tool's own output object, since each tool's output includes a dedicated summary field. I also need a done event carrying the final result or error, and need to check how tool-result content is wrapped to pull out the raw output and isError flag.\n\nRather than parsing runtime events for progress, I'll instrument each tool's execute wrapper directly to emit started/finished events with summaries, which is more robust and works cleanly with mocked agents in tests; I'll only subscribe to the agent itself for passthrough of assistant-message text. For the done criterion I'll check whether a save actually happened, retrying with a fresh agent if the first attempt doesn't produce one.\n\nFor the retry, I want to reset the session's proposal and test cases between attempts so stale state from a failed attempt doesn't confuse the next one, and I'm leaning toward structuring runToolAgent around a per-attempt builder function that constructs the tools and user message fresh each time.\n\nI should pass inputForHash explicitly rather than calling attempt(1) early just for hashing. I'm now tracing where the AgentTool type should come from—checking whether @cline/sdk re-exports it through core, since importing @cline/agents directly would be fragile as it's not a declared dependency.\n\nI'm sketching the dependency injection shape for createAgent in ai.ts, with a default implementation lazily importing Agent from the SDK, and noting that dynamic ESM imports of external packages should work fine under Turbopack given serverExternalPackages. I still need to verify a few SDK behaviors: how tool errors surface back to the model, and what happens when maxIterations is hit.\n\nFix #0 is complete. Before building T3, I'll verify a few SDK details—whether `AgentTool` is exported, how `completesRun`/`maxIterations` work, tool error handling, and the `node:sqlite` types.\n\n"}}
