{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:19:20.104Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I confirm createTool just converts the schema to JSON Schema without validating input before execute, and since shared's zod and the project's hoisted zod are the same instance, the instanceof check works fine. I'm also noting the defaults: a 30 second timeout, retryable true, and max retries of 3.\n\nSince my tools return `{ok:false, errors}` instead of throwing, I want retryable set to false so saveProposal doesn't get invoked twice, meaning I need to handle input validation myself inside execute using safeParse. I'm weighing whether to expose the full SchemeRule JSON schema as the tool's input schema versus keeping the tool schema loose (like a passthrough object) and validating against SchemeRule internally, since the looser approach lets validation errors flow back to the model for iteration while still satisfying providers that require an object-typed schema.\n\nI also second-guess whether the zod module instance used by the shared JSON-schema converter matches ours given CJS/ESM differences, but since the test output came back correct regardless, it seems to work fine either way.\n\nAnother concern is that having the model re-emit an entire large SchemeRule (like Orunodoi's ~150 lines with Assamese text) risks subtle corruption of strings even for a tiny edit. It'd be safer to design the tool so it can accept a smaller patch rather than a full replacement for minimal rule changes.\n\nI'm designing proposeRule to accept a \"new\" or \"change\" mode with a draft shape, and implementing a pure resolveDraft function in core to merge patches against the current rule. To keep things consistent, I'm considering having runTests and saveProposal operate on that same draft shape rather than a raw rule, possibly storing the validated draft in session state so later calls can reference it.\n\nThis session-state approach reduces how much data the model needs to resend and guarantees the saved rule matches exactly what was tested, which is a useful trust property even though the spec's function signatures suggest passing the rule directly each time. I'm leaning toward having proposeRule store the current draft on success, then having runTests operate against that stored draft plus a regression suite.\n\nFor saveProposal, I need to enforce a minimum number of passing new tests before allowing a save — the spec mentions ≥3 for OAP specifically, so I'm considering requiring 3 new test cases for brand new schemes and 2 for changes, which fits the Orunodoi example of testing October and November amounts plus unchanged eligibility.\n\nI'll keep the function signatures aligned with the spec but trust re-running tests at save time rather than relying on cached results, and again re-verify tests against the current published regression suite at publish time so nothing stale slips through.\n\nPublish rules need the proposal status to be \"proposed,\" the version strictly greater than the latest published version for that scheme, and the tests to re-pass before anything commits. For regression suites, I need to combine the built-in code-based tests with the accumulated tests from every published version of that scheme — though I have to watch for cases where an older version's test legitimately breaks on a newer version if behavior intentionally changed.\n\nI'm designing the discriminated union schema for test cases, starting with the benefit-type test fields and expected outcomes.\n\nI'm also sketching built-in regression suites for sample schemes like PM-KISAN and Orunodoi, defining specific test cases with expected eligibility statuses and benefit amounts tied to particular dates.\n\nFor Orunodoi I need the model to craft the docs examples itself, drawing from the schema and current rule definitions shown in the regression suite. The test runner should compare expectations and output pass/fail with failure reasons and an actual summary per case. I'm also setting up code-enforced policy checks on proposals — verifying id/version consistency for changes versus new rules, and checking provenance fields.\n\nFor provenance, any new or changed verified-status entry must point to a sourceUrl that literally appears in the notice text, otherwise it should error out demanding a provisional status unless an official URL is given — though I'm wrestling with whether a government portal like vikaspedia counts as \"official\" enough, since the code can only check presence of the URL in text, not judge its authority.\n\nFor the diff utility, I'm planning a deep comparison function that walks two JSON values and emits entries marking each path as added, removed, or changed, matching array items by identifying keys like id or docType so the output stays readable.\n\nNow I'm sketching the SQLite store: deciding on the database file location (configurable via an env variable, defaulting to a data directory, using in-memory for tests), making sure that path is gitignored, and defining the rule_versions table schema with columns for the scheme id, version, rule JSON, test JSON, results, and status.\n\nI'll store reviewer notes in notesJson. For versioning, I'm weighing a partial unique index on schemeId+version restricted to proposed/published statuses, so rejected proposals free up that slot — but then duplicate pending proposals for the same version would collide, forcing the agent to bump versions awkwardly during repeated demo runs, so I should narrow the uniqueness constraint further.\n\nOn the \"built-in schemes are version 1\" question, I'll treat the built-in packs as the baseline entry in version history (shown as \"built-in\" at 2.0.0) rather than literally renumbering them to 1.0.0, since that would break other assumptions.\n\nRather than inserting built-ins into the database, I'll merge them at the history level — combining built-in entries from the registry code with DB rows when listing a scheme's history.\n\nNow I'm sketching the store module's interface: a row type capturing scheme version metadata, rules, tests, status, and review notes, plus functions to open the store and insert, fetch, list, and update proposal statuses.\n\nI'm thinking through how to keep the store module the sole owner of DB access while making status transitions atomic. Since validation logic (re-running tests, checking version ordering) belongs elsewhere, I want the store to expose something like `publishProposal(id, at, validate)` that wraps a BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction around reading the row, running the caller's validation against the published set, and committing the update — relying on SQLite being synchronous and Node single-threaded to guarantee atomicity.\n\nI'm also working out how persisted JSON should be validated on read using the core schemas, and now I'm hitting a trickier issue: during `next build` with cacheComponents, a page that reads from the DB synchronously could get prerendered as static unless I explicitly await `connection()` first to force it dynamic, which raises the question of whether the root page needs Suspense to avoid a blocking-route build error.\n\nI'm sketching a solution where the page wraps an async `Catalog` component in Suspense — that component awaits `connection()`, syncs published rules, then renders the app, with a lightweight fallback shown during the wait. I'm also noting that `/api/check` needs `syncPublishedRules()` called first too, but since route handlers and pages might live in separate module registries, state might not be shared between them.\n\nNow I'm realizing this is critical: each server bundle has its own in-memory registry, so every entry point (API route, page, admin route) must independently sync published rules from the DB, since the DB is the real source of truth across bundles. `syncPublishedRules()` needs to be idempotent, checking if a version is already registered before adding it to avoid duplicate-registration errors, and should skip and log any published row that fails schema parsing rather than crashing.\n\nI should also check how `node:sqlite` behaves under Turbopack bundling and in Vitest — it's a newer builtin that might trigger experimental warnings or resolution issues, so I'll want to test that early. For concurrency, each process opening its own DB connection means multiple bundles in the same process could end up with separate connections, which I need to think through.\n\nFor the AI gateway, the agent's tool calls go through zod validation, and the final proposal comes from a structured `saveProposal` call rather than trusted free text. I need one retry on failure, but only if no proposal was actually saved yet — otherwise a retry could create a duplicate.\n\nSo I'm designing `runToolAgent` to take a prompt version, tools, and an `isDone` check, retrying once when the first attempt either fails or completes without saving, reusing the same session state across attempts. For testability, I want `ai.ts` to accept an injectable agent creator so I can mock it.\n\nI'm leaning toward lazily importing `@cline/sdk` inside the default factory so that lightweight routes and tests don't eagerly load the heavy SDK with its hub and sqlite dependencies. But I'm now worried that bundling `@cline/sdk` directly in Next via Turbopack could run into trouble with native or node-specific dependencies, so I'm weighing whether to import `@cline/agents` instead, even though the project conventions point to `@cline/sdk`.\n\nI'm checking whether `createTool` needs a static import in ruleMaintenance.ts since tools are built with it, which means the SDK loads at test time too—that's an acceptable ~1.5s cost. Keeping that static import there while the Agent class elsewhere uses the lazy import route seems like the right split.\n\nGood, so ai.ts stays the only module calling models directly, while ruleMaintenance just assembles tools and prompts and delegates to runToolAgent. For the propose endpoint, I want it to stream NDJSON events back to the client—tool-started/tool-finished events with input/output summaries, text chunks, and a final done or error event, all readable via a stream reader on the client side.\n\nRather than parsing SDK tool-result events to build summaries, it's simpler to have each tool emit its own human-readable summary through a session callback (e.g. \"5/5 passed, 3 new, 2 regression\"), plus a start event for liveness. Assistant text comes from mapping the SDK's assistant-message events into a simplified AgentEvent type, keeping ai.ts's onEvent passthrough generic.\n\nI'm now checking timeout and iteration defaults—180s per API call, 24 max iterations, 30s tool timeout—which should be plenty for a full scheme generation run of 1-3 minutes, and double-checking how temperature and other modelOptions are typed in the agent config schema.\n\nFor the demo notices feature, I'm weighing whether to read the sample markdown files from the docs directory at request time via fs in a server component, or instead duplicate that content into a TypeScript module to avoid filesystem access issues with caching and dynamic rendering.\n\nBut I also realize those doc files contain an \"Expected agent behaviour\" section alongside the actual notice text, which would leak the answer if loaded wholesale -- I need to extract just the notice paragraph, not the whole document.\n\nFor the IGNOAPS doc specifically, the source URL sits in an italic header line before the first separator, so a naive \"strip everything before the first `---`\" approach would lose the URL and cause the agent to mark it provisional incorrectly. The safer extraction is to drop just the title line and the \"Expected agent behaviour\" section, keeping the rest intact including the source URL line.\n\nI'll write `noticeFromDoc` as a pure function in `src/core/notice.ts` with accompanying tests. The agent should also receive today's date as `asOfDate` for computing `verifiedOn`, and the system prompt (versioned \"rule-maint-v1\") needs to specify the tools-only workflow starting with schema and current rule lookups.\n\nI'm defining how to decide new vs. changed schemes based on existing scheme IDs, using minimal JSON merge patches for changes with proper version bumps, and setting provenance rules — sourceUrl only when present, provisional status unless an official government URL appears, plus clause quoting, effectiveDate, and verifiedOn fields. I also need to make sure the agent never invents criteria, instead logging ambiguities as reviewNotes, and I'm thinking through how Assamese strings should be handled.\n\nDraft Assamese fields need a \"needs native-speaker review\" badge in the admin UI, and I'm planning at least three new test cases per scheme covering date boundaries and eligibility edge cases. For IGNOAPS specifically, I realize the benefit model only supports a single centralShare amount, which can't capture the age-tiered ₹200/₹500 structure — so the agent needs another way to represent that.\n\nI could make max optional in AgeRange to mean \"no upper limit,\" but that touches core engine logic and tests, which feels out of scope. Instead I'll keep the schema as-is and just have the agent use a practical upper bound like 120 for \"or above\" cases, clearly documented as a modeling choice rather than an invented criterion.\n\nFor stateTopUp I'd leave it null since the contribution is unconfirmed, and I'll add a scheme note about the ₹500 at age 80+, with a test checking centralShare 200 and total null. For the doc and verification slots, I should follow the nsap-widow scheme as a template — aadhaar for mandatory docs and name reference, bank passbook for name checks, dobFrom aadhaar for age verification, bpl_household verified — and have getRuleSchema return the full nsap-widow rule as the closest example.\n\nI'm also considering whether generating the JSON schema via z.toJSONSchema will work cleanly, since it ignores refinements and might choke on certain custom types, though IsoDate and url fields should convert fine. Since the full schema output would be huge with repeated provenance structures, I'm thinking getRuleSchema should return a compacted version that reuses shared definitions instead of duplicating them.\n\nFor testing, I'm planning to unit-test the tool functions directly against an in-memory store by calling execute with constructed inputs, then separately test the orchestration logic with a mocked agent that scripts out a sequence of tool calls.\n\nOn admin access, I'm weighing whether to add a lightweight optional token guard via an env var, but deciding to leave it as a noted open issue to keep scope manageable for the demo. I'm also flagging that agent runs have real costs, so the propose endpoint should probably have some rate limiting.\n\nI'll add a simple in-process lock so only one agent run executes at a time, returning a 429 if busy — cheap and effective. For publishing, the citizen-facing page syncs from the DB per request, so once a scheme is published the catalog picks up the newest version and `/api/check` resolves against it correctly, since the registry always resolves to the highest semver and sample data stays limited to built-in schemes.\n\nFor the benefit card, I want it to show the base amount via SchemeView while the report reflects `benefitOn(asOfDate)` — so with the October override in place, checking on 2026-10-10 should surface ₹1,500 with an isOverride flag and note, matching the demo date. I still need to verify BenefitCard actually renders that override note.\n\nFor diffing, I'll compute it on read rather than at save time: each proposal stores its `baseVersion`, and the diff against that version gets computed in the rules listing logic when the list is fetched. Writing test results schema...\n\nNow I'm sketching the RuleAdmin client component layout: a header with a back link, a notice textarea with demo-load buttons for the two sample scenarios plus a disables-while-running \"Run agent\" button, a live event log showing tool calls and assistant snippets, a proposals list (pending first) with scheme/version info, diff tables, grouped test results, and publish/reject actions gated on test status, plus a version history section per scheme.\n\nI'm also weighing language policy for the admin UI—since the project brief centralizes all fixed strings in a single i18n file with Assamese and English keys, I decide to add an \"admin\" section there too rather than hardcoding English-only strings, keeping the maintainer interface consistent with that rule even though it's meant for helper-org staff rather than citizens.\n\nNow I want to check how BenefitCard and the report view handle displaying overridden benefits.\n\n"}}
