{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:22:41.777Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Both test frameworks work with vitest. Now I'm defining the schema for benefit test cases, including id validation, description constraints, and the expected outcome structure.\n\nContinuing to design the evaluate test case schema with docs, answers, and expected eligibility/readiness/checks, then combining both into a discriminated union keyed on kind. I'm also planning the runRuleTest function that executes a test against a scheme, comparing actual results against expectations and collecting failures, wrapped in error handling.\n\nI'm also thinking through policy requirements — deciding that saveProposal should require at least 3 new test cases to encourage meaningful validation, and working out what counts as the \"existing regression suite\" for a scheme during regression testing.\n\nI realize there's a tricky edge case: a legitimate permanent change to a scheme (like a benefit amount update) could break an old regression test that was correct under the previous rules. Since the Orunodoi override is time-bounded, this particular demo avoids the conflict, but I need to define how regression suites accumulate across published versions so genuine rule changes aren't blocked by stale tests.\n\nOne option is letting the agent mark certain old tests as superseded with a reason, but that risks becoming a loophole for dodging failures rather than a legitimate fix, even with human review. Given time constraints, I'll leave this as a documented open issue rather than building it out now, and keep the regression suite scoped to the builtin plus accumulated published tests, independent of scheme versioning.\n\nFor the built-in regression suites, I'm sketching sample cases per scheme: pm-kisan needs eligible/review/action-required cases plus benefit and income-tax scenarios, while orunodoi-3 needs date-based benefit checks that avoid October dates so they don't collide with the upcoming October override being added.\n\nI also need to verify orunodoi's eligibility outcome for a clean-document case — rather than guessing whether provisional answers yield \"likely_eligible\" or \"needs_verification,\" I should write the regression test and run it to confirm the actual computed values match expectations.\n\nNow I'm sketching ruleDiff.ts: a diffRules function comparing a possibly-null \"before\" rule against an \"after\" rule, producing structured diff entries with path, kind (added/removed/changed), and before/after values. It needs to do a deep JSON diff, matching array items by id or docType to avoid noise from reordering, and truncate large object values to short JSON strings — with a special summary case when there's no prior rule at all.\n\nStill writing diff logic...\n\nFor the new-scheme case, each top-level key gets marked \"added\" with a readable summary—arrays become item counts with labels, objects get summarized by picking known label fields like labelEn or docType, falling back to compact truncated JSON when nothing matches.\n\nNow I'm sketching the policy checker: it validates that a proposed rule's version is strictly higher than the current one, allows the rule id to represent either an existing or new scheme, and walks through all provenance objects in the proposal to verify that anything marked \"verified\" is properly backed.\n\nA verified entry passes if it's unchanged from the current rule's provenance, or if its sourceUrl actually appears in the submitted notice text (as with the IGNOAPS case, where a government portal URL counts as sufficient backing). I'm working out how to compare provenance objects against the current rule without needing exact path matching, probably by comparing serialized JSON representations.\n\nBut this raises a problem: if the agent bumps the pack version on carried-over provenance fields, it won't match the original and will need a URL from the notice. Orunodoi's existing verified entry points to a government domain not mentioned in the notice, so the agent must leave that provenance untouched — meaning I need the system prompt to explicitly require byte-identical copying of unchanged fields, though having the model reproduce an entire 14KB rule JSON across multiple tool calls is expensive and error-prone, so a better design might avoid that entirely.\n\nI'm leaning toward a stateful draft approach: proposeRule stores a validated draft in the session, runTests runs against that stored draft, and saveProposal persists draft plus tests, with rule/tests parameters made optional so they default to the last valid draft when omitted — keeping compatibility with the original spec signatures while avoiding redundant large payloads.\n\nFor the actual change mechanism, I'm weighing full rule replacement against a patch-based approach, since asking the model to reproduce an entire ~8KB Assamese-language rule object just to bump a version and append a benefit override wastes tokens and risks transcription errors. A JSON merge-patch style update (schemeId plus a small patch object) seems far more efficient than requiring a full copy, especially given the output length that would otherwise be needed.\n\nFor \"new\" mode the rule must be complete, while \"patch\" mode applies a JSON merge patch where arrays replace entirely and null deletes fields. I'm deciding the response should return just a readable diff plus validation errors capped at 30 entries rather than the full merged rule, since that's more useful and keeps payloads small.\n\nI also need to enforce policy: new-mode on an existing scheme should error telling the agent to patch instead, patch-mode on an unknown scheme errors too, proposals must produce a non-trivial diff, and saveProposal requires at least three new tests with all tests—new and regression—passing before publish allows it.\n\nEach agent run gets a `RuleSession` holding the notice, draft rule, draft base, last tests, last run report, and saved id, with tools closing over this session. I'm splitting things so the actual business logic lives in plain functions in `src/server/rules.ts`, keeping it SDK-free for easier unit testing, while the tool wrappers live in `src/server/agents/ruleMaintenance.ts`.\n\nI'm weighing whether tests should exercise the tool wrappers directly or the underlying rule functions, and whether the agent orchestration can be tested with a fake Agent factory to avoid real network calls. I'm also second-guessing whether importing `@cline/sdk` into the test environment or Next's bundler will cause issues, since it pulls in a lot of Node-specific code through `@cline/core`.\n\nGiven that, I decide to keep `@cline/sdk` as the direct dependency but mark it along with `@cline/core` as `serverExternalPackages` in next.config so Next lets Node handle the native require/import at runtime instead of bundling it, since it's ESM-only and Next's server supports external ESM via dynamic import.\n\nThe agent wrapper itself needs to live in ai.ts: a `runToolAgent` function handling config checks, logging, agent creation (injectable for tests), event subscription, and execution. I'm now wrestling with whether to retry failed runs automatically, since a naive retry risks double-executing side-effecting tools like saveProposal if they already ran before the failure.\n\nThe safest rule is to only retry if the agent failed before any tool call occurred — otherwise just return a failure with whatever partial state exists, and let the code layer decide acceptance based on the saved proposal passing policy checks. I also need to pull in `Agent` and `createTool` from the SDK via dynamic import, where `createTool` is a simple zod-to-JSON-schema conversion used when defining the rule maintenance tools.\n\nStatically importing `createTool` in that module should be fine since it just loads the core SDK at module load time, which is acceptable both in the route handler and in tests given the import cost. I'm also checking whether the `AgentTool` type export covers what I need in ai.ts, and since I'm unsure the SDK exposes a runtime event type, I'll just define my own minimal event type locally instead.\n\nI'll map SDK events into my own `AgentLogEvent` union covering tool-call, tool-result, text, and error cases, typing the subscribe listener loosely by deriving it from the Agent's subscribe signature so I don't need to rely on an uncertain SDK export. For logging, each tool call should show the tool name with a short input summary and a result summary like pass/fail counts.\n\nI'll write pure summary helpers in `agentLog.ts` for tool calls and results, then build a streaming propose route that reads the request body and returns NDJSON lines of events ending in a done message, consumed on the client via a stream reader. I'm now considering how route handlers interact with cacheComponents prerendering—since the POST route won't be prerendered, but a GET route hitting the database with synchronous node:sqlite could actually complete fully during prerendering instead of bailing out.\n\nThis means I need to explicitly call `await connection()` at the start of any GET handler or page component that touches the registry or DB, so Next.js treats it as dynamic rather than baking in stale data. For the home page, I'll wrap the catalog-rendering component in Suspense, make it async, await the connection inside, sync published rules, and render the app—with a simple fallback while it streams in, rather than relying on a client-side fetch workaround.\n\nFor the admin page, I'm considering keeping the page itself static by rendering a server shell that passes in demo notices read from markdown docs at build time via `fs.readFileSync`, since those files live in the repo and can be baked into the static HTML; the actual rule data would then be fetched client-side by an `AdminApp` component on mount, avoiding any DB access during prerendering.\n\nI shouldn't expose the \"Expected agent behaviour\" guidance to the paste box—the admin should only see what's actually sent as the notice text, which sits between the `---` delimiters, though I want to preserve the \"Source: URL\" line from the header for provenance (important for the IGNOAPS doc). I'm sketching out a `noticeFromMarkdown` function that locates the `---` separator lines and extracts the body between the first and second occurrence (or to the end if there's no closing separator).\n\nFor the Orunodoi case, there's no source URL to prepend since the header just references the Economic Times report, so the body is just that one paragraph with the \"expected agent behaviour\" section excluded.\n\nNow I'm thinking about where the admin API handlers live—`src/server/adminApi.ts` with thin route handlers for proposing, listing, publishing, and rejecting rules. I'm flagging that having no authentication on publish/reject is a real gap even for a demo, so I should at least add a minimal optional `ADMIN_TOKEN` env check.\n\nSince adding full token-based auth would require UI changes outside the current scope, I'm logging this as an open issue rather than implementing it now. I'm sketching out the SQLite-backed store module, defining a `RuleVersionRow` interface with fields for schemeId, version, ruleJson, testsJson, testResultJson, status, sourceNote, timestamps, and optional baseVersion/noticeHash columns per the spec.\n\nI'm deciding to keep `approvedAt` specific to publish decisions and add `baseVersion` so history diffs can be computed by comparing against the referenced base scheme version at read time. Now I'm planning the store's writer API, with an `openStore` function and a singleton `getStore()` accessor that reads its database path from an environment variable.\n\nFor the core mutations, I'm designing `insertProposal` to run as a transaction that rejects duplicate scheme+version pairs unless the existing row was rejected, and also checks against the built-in rule registry to prevent colliding with reserved versions. I'm also sketching `listVersions`, `getVersion`, and a `publish` function that enforces the row be in `proposed` status before transitioning it.\n\nI need to make sure published versions stay strictly increasing per scheme, with `reject` flipping proposed rows to rejected and `listPublished` returning results ordered by id. I'm enabling WAL mode via a pragma (skipped for in-memory databases) and wrapping writes in explicit BEGIN IMMEDIATE/COMMIT/ROLLBACK transactions, keeping all database access confined to the store module.\n\nI'm also considering how `node:sqlite` behaves under Next.js's build tooling — Turbopack should externalize Node builtins properly, and since it's release-candidate status in Node 24, it likely won't throw experimental warnings.\n\nDuring build, pages that import the store without calling it at module scope shouldn't trigger a DB open, so I need to make sure the connection stays lazy. For the registry sync logic, I'm designing `syncPublishedRules` to iterate published rows in version order and register any scheme not already loaded, wrapped in try/catch for safety, and idempotent across calls — then wiring this sync into the homepage, the check API, and the admin API so newly published versions are always discoverable. Critically, `/api/check` must sync before calling `getScheme`, since different route bundles in Next might hold separate module instances of the in-memory registry, meaning a version published through one bundle could be invisible to another without this sync step.\n\nI'm worried this sync call inside `handleCheck` means importing the store (and thus opening the real SQLite file) during tests, since `apiCheck.test.ts` imports `api.ts` which now triggers sync on every check. To avoid tests touching the real database file, I'll configure vitest to set `KAAGAZ_DB_PATH` to an in-memory database via the test environment config. I also want to note that built-in schemes should be treated as version 1 per the spec.\n\nI'm thinking regression tests for a scheme should combine the builtin suite with tests from all published DB versions in publish order, deduped by id, with new test ids required to be unique against existing regression tests. For a brand-new scheme, I'm reasoning that since it can't affect other schemes, I don't necessarily need to rerun every other scheme's regression suite—just verify the new scheme's own tests pass.\n\nI should also validate that the new scheme's id doesn't collide with existing schemes, that question ids are unique within the scheme, and that referenced doc fields like dobFrom map to a doc type that actually carries a dob field in DOC_FIELDS.\n\nSimilarly, nameChecks and nameReference should require doc types with name-related fields. For IGNOAPS specifically, I'm thinking through how the agent should set the id, version, age range (60+ with no real max, so something like 120 or 150 would need to be used), and the tiered benefit amounts for 60-79 versus 80+.\n\nSince my Benefit model doesn't support age tiers, the agent should put the base 200 rupee amount in centralShare and note the 500 for 80+ separately rather than inventing a new field, with stateTopUp left null since states are only \"urged\" to contribute. I'm also mapping out the expected documents (aadhaar, bpl_proof, bank_passbook) and requirements (bpl_household), plus the test cases this should satisfy — eligible at 65 with BPL and all docs, ineligible at 55, ineligible without BPL, and action_required if proof is missing.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the actual workflow I need to follow: read the notice, check existing rules for relevant schemes, decide whether this is a new rule or a change, pull the schema, propose the rule, fix any validation errors, then run at least three tests covering the change and iterate until everything passes — treating any regression failure as a sign my rule is wrong, not the tests. Finally I'd save the proposal with a short sourced summary and reply concisely.\n\nFor provenance, I need to be careful: only include a sourceUrl if the notice actually gives one, quote the exact clause, mark status as provisional unless there's an official URL, and never invent criteria or amounts not stated in the text — anything ambiguous goes into a notes field. For new schemes I also need to draft Assamese text, using proper Assamese characters (ৰ and ৱ, not the Bengali র) and labeling them clearly as drafts needing review, which makes me think the code should actually validate that Assamese strings don't accidentally contain the Bengali character.\n\nI also need to remember: no file writing, only tool calls; version bumps follow semver (minor bump for changes, 1.0.0 for new schemes); and today's date comes from the user message via todayInIndia. Tests should follow the documented RuleTestCase schema with consistent check-id naming conventions like doc_, name_, age, req_, anyof, excl_, ext_, field_.\n\nNow I'm sizing the token budget: getRuleSchema returns roughly 14KB combining the schema, a built-in example like nsap-widow, and the test schema. I'm also setting an iteration cap of 24 and an overall timeout of about 5 minutes using an abort signal.\n\nI'm defining the tool agent's event types and prompt version for the runToolAgent function, covering tool calls, tool results, text output, and status events.\n\nI'm sketching the ToolAgentDeps and AgentLike interfaces, with a default createAgent that dynamically imports the SDK's Agent class, plus mapping the SDK's raw events (like \"tool-started\" and \"tool-finished\") into my own normalized tool-call and tool-result events.\n\nI'm also designing retry logic: if the agent's run fails and no tool calls occurred, retry once before returning an ai_failed error, and extending the logging schema's fn field to include \"runToolAgent\" alongside the existing \"extractDocument\" literal, plus considering new outcome values like \"failed\" or \"aborted\".\n\nI'm weighing whether to create a separate ToolAgentLogEntry type with its own log dependency rather than overloading the existing AiLogEntry union, which feels cleaner. I'm also planning to re-validate tool inputs with zod's safeParse inside each tool's execute function, since I can't be sure the SDK validates against the JSON schema beforehand.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the RuleSession interface for the server-side rules logic, tracking fields like notice, draft rule, test cases, and last run report, along with a getRuleSchemaInfo helper that exposes the JSON schemas and existing scheme metadata.\n\nI'm working through proposeDraft's validation logic: fetching the current published scheme, handling \"new\" mode (erroring if a base already exists) versus \"patch\" mode (erroring if no base exists and merging the patch against the current rule).\n\nI'm now handling runTestsFor, which accepts a set of test cases plus an optional proposal; if a proposal is supplied it first tries proposeDraft and bails on errors, otherwise it falls back to using the session's existing draft or errors out telling the caller to call proposeRule first.\n\nFor saveProposalFor, I'm requiring at least three test cases, rerunning them fresh to confirm everything passes, and only then persisting the proposal via the store and recording its id on the session.\n\nFor publishProposal and rejectProposal, I'm wiring up the store operations plus sync, with publish re-running the tests and regression suite against the scheme and enforcing a strictly higher version before success. For listRuleHistory, I'm structuring the output per scheme with current/built-in version info and per-version test summaries and diffs, while noting that testsJson only stores the agent's new tests and testResultJson captures the full combined report at save time.\n\nFor the regression suite composition, I'm merging built-in regression tests with previously published tests for that scheme, and confirming that competing proposals at the same version will simply fail publish rather than needing extra ordering checks.\n\nNow I'm turning to the admin UI — the server page reads demo notices from the filesystem at build time and passes them into the AdminApp component, and I'm weighing whether an English-only admin interface is acceptable given that the i18n requirement technically calls for all UI strings to live in the i18n file with both language variants.\n\nI'm sketching the admin page structure: an English-locked root, a scheme notice input with demo-load buttons and a run trigger, a live agent log showing tool calls and results, and a proposal review section pulling from the rules API to display version diffs and source notes.\n\nI'm adding to that review the colored diff table, test results split into new and regression checks, publish/reject buttons that refresh the list and surface a \"now live\" confirmation, plus a version history panel with status badges that can reopen proposals for review. The rules API response shape nests schemes with their versions, each carrying status, timestamps, and test results.\n\nFor agent log summaries, I'm designing pure helper functions that convert tool inputs and outputs into concise human-readable strings server-side, so streamed events only carry lightweight summaries like `{type, id, name, summary}` rather than full tool payloads reaching the browser.\n\nI'm also marking `@cline/sdk` as an external package since it needs runtime requires from node_modules, and sketching a smoke-test script that hits the live propose API with real demo notices, printing the streamed events and resulting proposal to verify the whole pipeline works end-to-end.\n\nI'll add a `--publish` flag that runs the full end-to-end check: publish both schemes, then call /api/check to confirm Orunodoi's October-vs-November benefit difference (1500 vs 1250) and verify the new scheme appears correctly with its scheme ID and name on the homepage. I'll verify this against a real local server run with the actual API key, keeping in mind the published versions will persist in the demo database afterward.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the test suite: rule evaluation tests covering pass/fail cases and invalid dates, a regression suite ensuring every built-in scheme passes its own tests with unique IDs, and a rule-diff test verifying that identical versions produce no diff while benefit overrides and new schemes are detected correctly.\n\nI'm also mapping out mergePatch tests (objects merge, arrays replace, nulls delete, no mutation), rulePolicy tests (version must increase, verified notices need URLs, Bengali script rejected, dobFrom requires dob, no duplicate question ids, unchanged rules rejected), and notice tests that pull real doc content using fs alongside inline string fixtures.\n\nI'm then sketching agentLog tests for summaries, store tests covering insert/list/publish/reject transitions with duplicate-version and bad-transition rejections plus WAL pragma checks, and rules tests that run full propose-to-publish flows on both the Orunodoi patch and the new IGNOAPS rule, including failure paths where unpassed tests block saving and regressions block patches.\n\nSince the registry is module-global but isolated per test file, I need to be careful that sequential tests publishing the same scheme version don't conflict -- I'm planning to make publish sync skip re-registering if a version already exists, and to structure the rules test as one sequential describe block with increasing version numbers rather than reusing the same version across independent tests.\n\nNext, for the agent module I'm planning tests for `createRuleTools` (verifying tool names, JSON schema inputs, and execution wiring back into rules.ts) and for `runRuleMaintenanceAgent`, mocking the AI module so a simulated model can call tools in sequence.\n\nBeyond that I need coverage for `aiAgent.test.ts` (unconfigured AI handling, event mapping, retry logic for failed tool calls, no leaked input in logs) and `adminApi.test.ts` (NDJSON streaming, history listing, publish/reject status codes, validation errors). This is a lot of ground to cover, so I'll start writing it in manageable chunks, beginning with the core rule tests file.\n\nI should check the existing evaluate test file to see what actual outputs the engine produces, so I can set realistic expectations in the regression suite rather than guessing.\n\nGood news: zod's JSON Schema export and node:sqlite both work fine under Vitest. Now I'll review the existing Orunodoi and widow pension engine tests to reuse their fixtures and expected results before writing the regression suites.\n\n"}}
