{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:26:24.294Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm designing the core module structure, starting with a schema for rule test cases that defines a discriminated union covering benefit scenarios with expected central share, state top-up, and total values.\n\nI'm also defining an \"evaluate\" test type that validates eligibility, readiness, and per-check results against extracted documents and answers. I'm adding a `runRuleTests` function to execute cases, compare expected versus actual outcomes, catch errors as failures, and a `summarize` helper to tally passed/failed counts, plus built-in regression tests for schemes like PM-KISAN with sample documents mapped to expected review statuses.\n\nI need to be careful with the Orunodoi regression tests since a new October benefit override could break an old test that expects a flat 1250 amount on a date within October 2026 -- so I should pick a date outside that window, like mid-September, to keep old assertions valid while avoiding asserting benefit amounts in evaluate-type tests where it's not needed.\n\nThinking about whether a legitimately conflicting regression test should be replaceable by a new proposal with matching id -- that feels like a loophole for the agent to rewrite tests to force a pass, so safer to keep regression tests immutable and have the tool reject any id collision outright.\n\nFor the nsap-widow case, sample C should map to action_required needing a death certificate doc, while someone aged 39 should be not_eligible with a flat 300 benefit and no top-up.\n\nOn storage, I'm deciding that when a proposal publishes, its tests join the scheme's permanent regression suite, so `getCurrentRule` returns both the current rule and the full accumulated test history across versions -- meaning older tests like the October Orunodoi case stay valid unless a later notice explicitly changes that period again, and brand-new schemes like IGNOAPS simply start with just their own tests. Now I'm sketching the `diffRules` function that compares a scheme's rule before and after a change and emits a list of diff entries marking paths as added, removed, or modified.\n\nFor arrays I want to key by id or docType when possible and fall back to index, with paths written in a readable form and values truncated to short summaries. For brand-new schemes where \"before\" is null, I'm treating it as an empty object so every top-level field just shows up as \"added,\" with array fields like mandatoryDocs summarized as something like \"3 items: aadhaar, bp...\" rather than dumping the full JSON.\n\nWriting the recursive diff walker...\n\nI'm designing a policy checker that validates proposed scheme rules against the current version: it enforces that version numbers must increase, and that any \"verified\" provenance entries must either match an unchanged entry from the current rule or have their source URL actually present in the notice text, so claims can't be marked verified without backing evidence.\n\nFor gathering provenance objects, I'll walk the structure generically to find anything with the right status and verification fields, rather than hardcoding specific paths.\n\nI also need to export `compareVersions` from the schemes registry so the policy module can reuse it, keeping it in the same index file rather than splitting it out.\n\nNow thinking through `extractNoticeText`: the demo markdown files put the source URL in an italic preamble before the first `---`, but the Orunodoi sample also has an \"Expected agent behaviour\" section after the content — feeding that to the agent would be cheating since it's documentation metadata, not actual notice content I should extract.\n\nI'm designing a pure function that splits the markdown on `---` lines, takes the body between the first and second divider, then scans the preamble for a \"Source: <url>\" pattern via regex and appends it to the body text if found.\n\nFor the Orunodoi notice, there's no source URL—just a caveat about confirming against the official government notification, so the sourceUrl stays null while the demo notice remains clearly provisional.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how the admin API route reads the docs folder at runtime using the filesystem, which works fine for `next start` in a local build.\n\nI'll move the demo notice reading into a dedicated server module using a synchronous file read. For the agent log, I'll have each tool return a summary string directly rather than building a separate summarization module—simpler to just include a `summary` field in tool outputs that gets streamed as `{type:\"tool\", name, summary, ok}` events to the client. Now I'm setting up the store module...\n\nI need to define row and record types for rule versions, tracking schema id, version number, rule JSON, test results, status, and timestamps. For diffs, rather than storing them, I'll compute them on read by comparing against whatever version was published at proposal time—so I should store a `baseVersion` reference to make that comparison possible.\n\nI'm also weighing where agent notes and unclear items should live; rather than cramming them into testResultJson, it seems cleaner to handle unclear items through the SchemeRule's own notes field with provenance, or possibly add a dedicated field for open questions.\n\nFor the schema itself, I'm adding a `reviewNotes` column storing a JSON array of things the agent couldn't resolve, alongside id, scheme/version info, rule and test JSON, status, timestamps, and a uniqueness constraint on scheme+version that I'm making partial—excluding rejected proposals—so rejected attempts don't block reuse of that version number.\n\nNow I'm sketching the Store API: an `openStore`/`getStore` singleton backed by a configurable DB path (with `:memory:` for tests), plus functions for inserting a proposal, listing versions, fetching a version by id, and publishing a version inside a transaction that validates it's currently in `proposed` status before marking it published. I'm also considering whether publishing should enforce that the new version number is strictly greater than whatever is currently published.\n\nSince node:sqlite calls are synchronous, there's no risk of interleaving within a single operation, so I can keep the store itself simple and push version-comparison logic into the higher-level `publishProposal` function in rules.ts: fetch the record, validate the rule parses, compare against the currently registered scheme version, and reject as stale if the proposal's version isn't newer than what's already active.\n\nI also want to re-run the tests at publish time as a cheap deterministic safety check before actually committing the UPDATE, using a conditional WHERE clause on status='proposed' plus a changes===1 check to guarantee atomicity. I also need a `reject(id, now)` method, with `now` injected as an ISO string for testability, and I'm weighing whether node:sqlite being an experimental Node builtin will cause issues with Turbopack bundling.\n\nNode 24 should handle it fine without flags, so I'll verify at build time. For the DB path I'll use `data/kaagaz.db` and make sure `/data/` is gitignored, while being careful that no module opens the database eagerly at import time during `next build` — I'll lazily open it on first use and make the `/` page dynamic so it reads from the DB at request time inside a Suspense boundary. Now I'm writing the registry sync function that iterates published records and registers any scheme not already present.\n\nI'm realizing this registry sync matters because the in-memory registry Map is module-level, and in Next.js, pages versus route handlers may run in separate module graphs/layers (rsc vs route-handler) that don't necessarily share instantiated module state — so I need to make sure the sync call happens on every relevant request rather than assuming the registry persists across routes.\n\nI need to call `syncRegistry()` at the start of each consumer: the homepage flow, `/api/check`, and admin APIs, so published changes take effect immediately. But since `handleCheck` lives in `src/server/api.ts`, adding a DB sync there risks tests writing to the real `data/kaagaz.db` file, so I should make the store path configurable via a `KAAGAZ_DB_PATH` env variable and set that in the vitest config to isolate tests from the production database.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the agent's publish flow: the agent tools work through a rules service that exposes a schema endpoint returning the JSON schema for scheme rules, test case format, vocabulary, and a compact example rule, plus a way to fetch the current rule state for a given scheme.\n\nI also need proposeRule to validate a submitted rule against both the schema and policy and return structured errors, and runTests to validate a rule and its test cases before execution -- which means the tool's input schema for the rule itself should stay loose so malformed agent input surfaces as readable validation errors rather than being rejected outright by the SDK. I'm now checking whether the SDK itself enforces JSON schema validation on tool inputs before execution reaches my code.\n\nLoose input validated internally gives clearer error messages. For runTests, I'll merge new cases with the regression suite tied to the rule id, returning per-case results tagged by origin plus a summary. For saveProposal, I'll validate the rule, policy, and test ids, run everything, and refuse to save if any test fails -- that way every saved proposal is guaranteed publishable.\n\nI should mark saveProposal's success as completing the run deterministically, but only on an actual successful save -- if validation fails I need to throw rather than return an ok:false object, since a thrown error correctly signals failure to the SDK while a returned object would still count as a \"successful call\" and end the run prematurely. I'll pair completesRun with a completion policy that nudges the model to call it if it stops early.\n\nI'm also second-guessing whether throwing from saveProposal is safe given the default retryable behavior with maxRetries 3 -- if the runtime automatically retries a thrown tool execution, that could re-invoke saveProposal multiple times unintentionally, so I need to check how that retry mechanism actually works before relying on throw for failure signaling.\n\nSince retries on validation failures would just be wasteful rather than harmful, I'm setting `retryable: false` on all tools to avoid that ambiguity entirely. I'm planning one agent run per propose request, with a `buildRuleTools` factory that closes over the notice text, store, and a save callback so the tools have the context they need, and the run should terminate as soon as a proposal is successfully saved.\n\nNow I'm turning to the `runToolAgent` function in ai.ts, keeping in mind the rule that every AI call must route through one module with structured JSON output, zod validation, a single retry, and a safe fallback, logging the model, prompt version, and input hash.\n\nWriting the Agent construction logic...\n\nIf isDone fails, I retry once with a fresh agent before falling back to an ai_failed error. The structured JSON output is really just the tool inputs validated by zod, so I should document that clearly, and deps.createAgent needs to be injectable so tests can mock the agent instead of loading the heavy @cline/sdk import at module scope.\n\nI'm weighing whether to dynamically import @cline/sdk inside the default factory so unit tests with injected createAgent never pull in the SDK, but ruleMaintenance.ts still statically imports createTool from @cline/sdk, which would load the whole core during tool tests anyway -- so I need to figure out whether that import can also be made dynamic without complicating the tool definitions.\n\nSince Turbopack would try bundling the heavy @cline packages into the server route, I should mark them as serverExternalPackages in next.config.ts so they're required at runtime instead. Then I'm drafting the agent's system prompt for rule maintenance, defining its role, constraints, and the step sequence starting with getRuleSchema.\n\nThe remaining steps: identify whether it's a new scheme or a change using getCurrentRule, build a complete proposed rule JSON with proper versioning (bumping minor for changes, starting at 1.0.0 for new kebab-case schemes), iterate with proposeRule until valid, then write at least three regression tests covering benefits and eligibility, running them until everything passes without weakening any test, before finally saving.\n\nI also need to include sourceNote and reviewNotes, track provenance carefully (sourceUrl only if present in the notice, status \"provisional\" unless there's an official government URL, with clause and verifiedOn fields), write the Assamese translations using proper script, and structure one-time or temporary benefit changes as overrides with date ranges rather than permanent edits.\n\nFor central vs state shares, I'm mapping central schemes to centralShare and state-level top-ups to stateTopUp, leaving it null when contributions are merely \"urged\" rather than confirmed. The trickier issue is IGNOAPS, where the benefit amount depends on age (₹200 for 60-79, ₹500 for 80+) but the schema only supports one amount per scheme — I'm resolving this by setting centralShare to 200 as the base, adding a note about the ₹500 increase at 80+, and setting an open-ended age range from 60 with no upper limit, then testing eligibility at age 85 against the base benefit value.\n\nI should also note bpl_household as a requirement, with confirmAt set to block_office, and make sure the schema output includes example test cases for both formats. I'll pass today's date to the agent via todayInIndia() so it has temporal context, and structure the user message with the notice text, date, and existing scheme IDs/names—keeping in mind token budget since the full SchemeRule JSON with Assamese strings could get long.\n\nActually, I'm worried about latency: if the orunodoi rule with its 11 Assamese questions needs to be reproduced three separate times across proposeRule, runTests, and saveProposal calls, that's potentially tens of thousands of output tokens and minutes of wait time, which is too slow for a demo. I should reconsider the design—maybe allow proposals to reference a stored draft rather than requiring the full JSON to be repeated on every call.\n\nFor an existing scheme, the output would be tiny overrides on the benefit field rather than a whole rewritten rule, matching \"smallest change\" intent — the stored rule still gets fully merged and zod-validated afterward. So I'll support both a full `rule` payload for new schemes and a `changes` payload of simple set operations with dot-paths and numeric array indices for edits to existing ones.\n\nI'm designing the `applyRuleChanges` core function to apply a list of set/remove/append operations to a base object and return either the merged value or an error, with tests covering it. Then the `proposeRule` tool wraps this: it builds the candidate rule, validates it, and stores it in an in-memory drafts map keyed by a generated draft ID, returning validation results and a diff summary.\n\nI'm sketching `runTests({ draftId?, rule?, testCases })` to evaluate a draft or inline rule against test cases, and `saveProposal({ draftId, tests, sourceNote, reviewNotes })` to persist the final result. For the IGNOAPS new-scheme case the agent needs to emit one large multilingual rule object, which is acceptable even if slow, while the Orunodoi override and evaluate test cases need bilingual documentation and provenance notes written alongside them.\n\nI'm now deciding the minimum test-count requirement for saveProposal — since Done-when criterion (a) specifies at least 3 passing tests for a new scheme, I'll enforce that threshold for new schemes while requiring a smaller minimum (2) for simple changes.\n\nNext I'm sketching the streaming endpoint for proposing rule changes: a POST route that accepts a notice string and responds with newline-delimited JSON events covering start, tool invocations, brief assistant notes, and a final proposal payload.\n\nI'm tracing how the agent runtime's internal events get translated into this neutral event shape—tool-start, tool-end, text, attempt—so rule maintenance logic can turn them into readable log lines with summaries.\n\nInstead of parsing raw SDK messages for tool results, I'm deciding it's simpler to have each tool emit its own start and end events via a closure callback, keeping error cases handled by emitting before any throw. This keeps the event stream consistent regardless of which tool is running.\n\nI'm also thinking ai.ts's onEvent should just forward assistant text and attempts, with a simple logged wrapper applied in ruleMaintenance. Model text between tool calls can surface as truncated \"note\" lines so the visible log shows intent like \"I will add an October override.\" For the admin API, I'm sketching the shape of GET /api/admin/rules, returning schemes with their current and builtin versions plus a list of rule versions including diffs, status, and review metadata.\n\nFor the diff, I'm deciding to compute it on read rather than storing a separate diff_json column -- fetch the base version via the registry after sync, treat a missing base as everything-added, and keep it cheap and simple. I also want built-in versions to appear in history, so each scheme response should include a builtin field with its version and name.\n\nI'm sketching publish and reject endpoints: publish re-validates, reruns tests, checks versioning, then calls store.publish and syncRegistry, returning either success or a 409 with a reason; reject just calls store.reject. Route params use the async params pattern typical of this framework.\n\nOn security, I'm flagging that the admin routes have no auth — acceptable for a hackathon demo but worth noting as an open issue since unauthenticated rule-changing endpoints could be abused, possibly suggesting an optional ADMIN_TOKEN env var as a future fix.\n\nFor pages, I'm planning the home page to be dynamic with a Suspense boundary wrapping the main app, ensuring the registry syncs on load with a minimal skeleton fallback, and the admin page as a server component surfacing demo notices.\n\nThe admin notices get read synchronously from disk at build time, which should complete during prerender and stay static, though Turbopack might just warn about dynamic fs paths. I'm deciding to keep the admin UI in English since it's a maintainer tool, unlike the citizen-facing Assamese default, while still showing Assamese content in diffs where relevant.\n\nNow I'm wrestling with the \"one src/i18n.ts\" rule requiring matching en/as keys—adding ~40 admin strings to both languages is heavy, and a separate strings file would violate that rule, so I'm leaning toward folding admin strings into i18n.ts under their own namespace despite the extra translation work.\n\nI'm also thinking through the override scenario: when the Orunodoi report publishes on the demo date, the citizen-facing BenefitCard should show the overridden ₹1,500 amount with a note, while the base SchemeStep still shows the original amount—a nice moment to demo how publishing propagates to citizen views.\n\nI'm weighing whether to add an optional `asOfDate` parameter to `catalogOf` so it can compute the current benefit via `benefitOn` and attach the override note, but I'm wary since catalog.ts is deliberately kept server-only and imports from the schemes registry, so I need to make sure this change doesn't leak engine logic into client-safe code.\n\nSince benefit.ts is small and pure, I decide it's safe to add, plus a test for it, keeping the scope modest. I'm also confirming that once the new IGNOAPS \"nsap-oldage\" 1.0.0 scheme is published, `listSchemes()` will surface it in the catalog automatically, with doc slots and questions derived from its requirements and existing DOC_LABELS covering all DocTypes.\n\nFor publish conflicts, I reason through the case where two proposals for the same scheme share a base version — the partial unique index should block a duplicate insert unless the first was rejected, so the agent just needs to bump the version and retry. I'm noting the built-in schemes are already at 2.0.0 rather than 1.0.0 as the spec implies, and deciding to just keep that as-is with a note.\n\nFor the regression suite, I'm working out that it should combine built-in regression tests with all published test cases from the store, deduplicated by unique test id since uniqueness is enforced at save time.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how running tests for a specific rule version applies the regression suite from published versions to the new version, and considering the shape of the test run report — including timestamp, totals, and per-test results with their origin. I also want the `/api/check` endpoint to sync the registry before fetching the scheme, calling a `syncPublishedRules()` helper from the rules module to keep the API handler testable.\n\nFor the storage layer, memory-based stores work fine in tests via environment configuration, and the SQLite setup handles in-memory databases correctly even though WAL journal mode doesn't apply there — transactions can be wrapped in a helper that issues BEGIN IMMEDIATE and COMMIT/ROLLBACK. For testing rule agents, I'm planning to make the underlying agent runner injectable so it can be mocked.\n\nFor integration tests, I want a fake agent implementation that simulates the model by calling tools directly in sequence — fetching the schema, pulling the current rule, proposing changes, running tests, and saving the proposal — then verifying the resulting stored record shows the expected pass counts. I also need unit tests for the tool-agent runner itself covering the no-config case, retry-then-success case, repeated-failure case, and confirming logs capture model info, prompt version, and input hash without leaking sensitive data.\n\nI'm defining `isDone` as whether the proposal was actually saved, with the agent given that check so it knows to retry once if it completes without saving — the retry costs extra time but satisfies the requirement, and tool state persisting across attempts is harmless. I also want to support aborting the agent if the client disconnects, wiring the request's abort signal through to the agent so a stream cancellation triggers a proper abort, and I'm accepting that the route itself has no built-in timeout, so an agent run might legitimately take a couple of minutes.\n\nI'm setting `maxIterations: 24` and relying on the default `apiTimeoutMs` of 180000, deciding to skip temperature since it's not clearly part of Agent config, and not worrying about max tokens per turn since the model/provider defaults should be generous enough for the expected output size. I confirmed the SDK recognizes the \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5\" model under the openai-compatible provider, and now I'm looking at how assistant message content shows up in the event stream so I can map it correctly in the onEvent handler.\n\nI'll forward text-type content parts and handle run-failed events by logging them. Then I'm planning the implementation order: starting with core pieces like rule tests, regression schemes, rule diffing, rule changes, rule policy, and notices (each with their own tests), followed by server-side work covering storage, rules, the tool agent runner, rule maintenance agent, admin API routes, and finally the admin UI and catalog changes.\n\nNext I need the smoke-test script, Next.js config, gitignore, and vitest environment setup. I'm resolving a naming discrepancy between the backlog's `src/server/agents/ruleMaintenance.ts` and the system patterns doc's `src/server/agent/`, deciding to go with the backlog's plural \"agents\" path.\n\nWriting ruleTests.ts...\n\nDefining more schema cases...\n\nDefining more types...\n\nI'm planning out the test runner functions that compare expected vs actual values for benefits and evaluations, generating readable failure messages like \"stateTopUp: expected 1500, got 1250\" or flagging checks missing from a report, plus wrapping execution in error handling.\n\nNow I'm sketching built-in regression suites for pm-kisan using sample data: checking readiness/eligibility outcomes for the clean sample, the name-mismatch sample, and the income-tax-disqualification case.\n\nThen I'm adding a benefit calculation test confirming the 6000 central share with no state top-up, and verifying SAMPLES imports stay core-only.\n\nFor orunodoi-3, I'm defining cases for a clean ready applicant, missing ration card, four-wheeler ownership triggering needs_verification, and starting a regular benefit test.\n\nMoving to nsap-widow, I'm setting up tests for a missing death certificate requiring action, an applicant under the eligible age threshold, and beginning to verify readiness for a complete documentation case.\n\nI should verify the widow_benefit_300 case with centralShare 300 and null totals, making sure the built-in regression suite passes against the built-in rules. Now I'm defining a RuleChange schema as a discriminated union over set/append/remove operations, with paths handled as dot-separated segments where all-digit segments map to array indices.\n\nI'm also allowing array items to be selected by id or docType when a segment isn't purely numeric, which makes paths more intuitive for an LLM to generate. Writing applyRuleChanges...\n\nI'm guarding against prototype pollution by rejecting path segments like \"__proto__\", \"constructor\", or \"prototype\", then moving to a rulePolicy module that validates proposed scheme rule changes—checking version bumps, verification status against current and proposed rules, and the notice text.\n\nFor test count validation, I'm setting minimum thresholds of 3 tests for new schemes and 2 for changes, then folding everything into a single proposalProblems function. For provenance, I'm recursively walking the proposed object to find entries with status, sourceTitle, and verifiedOn fields, then checking each verified entry has a sourceUrl that's referenced in the notice text, or matches an existing provenance record in the current rule via deep comparison.\n\nSince LLM re-emission can scramble object key order, I need a stable stringify with sorted keys rather than naive JSON.stringify comparison, and I'm crafting the error message to explain that verified rules need an official source URL appearing in the notice, falling back to \"provisional\" status otherwise, with the path included for clarity. I'm confirming the Orunodoi and IGNOAPS examples both work correctly under this scheme since their provenances either carry over unchanged or reference the vikaspedia URL present in the notice. Now I'm sketching a noticeFromMarkdown function that normalizes line endings and splits content into sections on horizontal rule markers.\n\nI'm handling the edge cases around how many sections appear, extracting a body and preamble accordingly, and searching the preamble for a \"Source:\" URL to append to the body if it's missing. I'm also setting a max character cap of 20000 for notices.\n\nFor ruleDiff.ts, I'm working out how a `show(value)` helper formats different types -- strings truncated at 160 chars, primitives stringified directly, keyed-object arrays rendered as bracketed lists of key values, and generic objects compacted to JSON truncated at 200 chars. I'm deciding to skip adding special labels for well-known paths, then moving on to actually writing these files along with exporting `compareVersions` from the schemes index and drafting ruleTests.ts.\n\n"}}
