{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:28:34.924Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm working out the code-enforced rule policy: proposals need a version bump over the current published one, and verified provenance claims are only valid if the source URL literally appears in the notice text or the provenance object exactly matches one already carried over from the current version.\n\nFor IGNOAPS, the notice cites a Vikaspedia government portal URL, which PM-KISAN already treats as acceptable for verified status, so that precedent likely applies here too. For Orunodoi, though, the notice lacks an official URL, so any new provenance should be marked provisional per the stated policy, while existing verified provenances carried unchanged from the current version remain valid exceptions.\n\nI'm wary that comparing provenance records to decide \"unchanged\" is tricky—should I ignore version and verifiedOn fields, or require an exact match? The real risk is a malicious edit that keeps an existing verified sourceUrl but applies it to a different, previously provisional criterion (like Orunodoi's four_wheeler exclusion), turning it into a definitive not_eligible improperly. So I should require the verified status and sourceUrl to be unchanged at the exact same path compared to the current published version, not just reused anywhere in the document.\n\nI'll write a helper that walks a rule and builds a path-to-provenance map, using the same keyed-path scheme as the diff logic, so I can check each verified entry against the equivalent path in the current rule. I also need to add validation that new scheme IDs don't collide and that version numbers always increase relative to both published and builtin versions.\n\nFor test requirements, I'll set a minimum of 3 new test cases per proposal to encourage quality coverage, matching the task's done-when criteria. New test IDs can't collide with existing regression IDs, and both saveProposal and publish must rerun the full regression suite to confirm everything still passes.\n\nNow I'm defining the regression set per scheme — built-in tests plus tests from all published versions, empty for brand-new schemes. But I'm hitting a real conflict: the existing oru_benefit_1250 regression test asserts 1250 on 2026-10-10 with no override, yet the October notice changes the amount for that exact date, meaning the new version would break that old regression test.\n\nSo I should pick a safer reference date for the built-in regression test, one that sits clearly before the notice period, like 2026-09-15 or 2026-09-01, framed as checking the \"regular amount\" rather than the overridden period — this way old tests keep passing as the done-when requires.\n\nI'm also thinking about the edge case where saveProposal should refuse if tests fail, forcing the agent to iterate rather than silently publishing a conflicting regression; that keeps the design strict and consistent with the \"iterate until valid\" requirement. Now let me go fix the actual regression date in the test.\n\nThe diff passes, but the existing Orunodoi benefit regression test uses a date inside the October override window, which the legitimate notice would break. I'll move that test to September so it checks the recurring amount unaffected by the override.\n\n"}}
