{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:31:14.353Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"My regex using a lazy capture with a trailing character class should correctly grab the URL while trimming the trailing asterisk, so that looks solid. I'll add a test later using real docs files, and now I'm moving on to designing the SQLite-based store module.\n\nI'm defining the rule store interface with types for version status, rows storing scheme rules, tests, results, and timestamps, plus methods for inserting proposals, listing, publishing, rejecting, and seeding built-ins. For seeding, I'm reconciling the spec's notion of \"version 1\" for built-ins with the fact that built-in schemes internally carry a pack version like \"2.0.0\" -- I'll treat the first row in the rule_versions history as version 1 regardless of the pack's own version label.\n\nFor uniqueness, I'm working through using a partial unique index on scheme and version that excludes rejected rows, so a rejected proposal frees up its version number for reuse while still preventing two simultaneously proposed rows from colliding on the same version.\n\nI'm also planning the transaction helper around the sqlite DatabaseSync API, wrapping begin/commit/rollback logic, and sketching the publish flow which needs to verify the row is still in \"proposed\" status and that no other published row exists before finalizing.\n\nFor the database path, I'm settling on storing it under `data/kaagaz.db`, gitignoring the folder, and allowing an environment override, plus enabling WAL mode for durability. I'm also deciding the store layer should validate data against the Zod schemas on read, not just write, to keep things safe.\n\nNow I'm thinking through whether the Next.js build will correctly externalize `node:sqlite` as a built-in, since Turbopack needs to recognize the `node:` prefix rather than trying to bundle it, and I want to verify this works before relying on it in the runtime.\n\nI also need to handle the fact that the store shouldn't open the database at module import time, since that would run during static prerendering. Instead I'll lazily open a singleton connection on first access, keep it on globalThis to survive dev HMR, make the root page dynamic with a Suspense boundary, and keep the admin page purely client-side so it only talks to the DB through API routes. I'm now sketching the server-side rules service that syncs the registry with the store.\n\nThat explains why syncing from the DB on every request is the robust approach. For regression tests, I'm thinking each scheme's test set should be the union of tests across all its published versions, deduped by id with the latest winning, and built-in schemes get seeded with BUILTIN_REGRESSION_TESTS so everything stays uniform—seeding happens by inserting a published row for each built-in scheme if none exists for that schemeId/version.\n\nThe actual test results come from runRuleTests, and I want to keep the store itself free of core logic beyond types, with seeding data passed in from rules.ts and inserted transactionally. The tricky part is that if the built-in tests change in code later, a previously seeded row would still have stale tests, so I'm leaning toward always upserting built-in rows' tests_json on seed so they stay in sync with the code-defined BUILTIN_REGRESSION_TESTS, while non-built-in published rows keep their own test sets.\n\nNow I'm sketching the shared service layer functions both the tools and API will call: validating a proposed rule, fetching the current rule and its regression tests for a scheme, computing combined regression tests, running a test report on a new rule against new and regression tests, and saving the result.\n\nFor publishing, I'm designing a gate where the code re-runs all tests against the latest regression set at publish time (in case another version was published in the meantime), requires every test to pass and the version number to be higher than current before persisting and registering the scheme. SaveProposal itself should enforce valid schema, policy compliance, and full test passage upfront so the agent iterates until the proposal is genuinely publishable, and I still need to work out the policy's notice text requirement.\n\nNow I'm deciding that the notice text should come from the agent's run context rather than from the model itself, so the tool closure injects it directly and the model can't fabricate it. For the agent tools, I'm keeping the input schemas loose -- using generic record/array types for rule and tests rather than a fully strict zod schema -- and instead doing the real validation inside the tool's execute function with SchemeRule and RuleTestCase parsers, returning readable per-item errors back to the model.\n\nI'm working out that getRuleSchema should expose the JSON-schema version of SchemeRule plus the test case schema plus a concrete example rule and example tests, since refinements get dropped in JSON schema conversion but that's fine. For outputs, getCurrentRule just returns the rule JSON directly, and proposeRule returns either a success object with version and diff summary, or a failure object listing formatted zod issues (capped around 30) alongside any policy violations.\n\nI'm realizing the policy check needs splitting since test-count rules don't apply here — better to break rulePolicy into separate checkRulePolicy and checkTestPolicy functions, with checkProposalPolicy composing both. Then runTests validates the rule and test cases together and reports pass/fail counts with failure details, and saveProposal just returns the resulting proposal id.\n\nNow I'm debating whether saveProposal should mark completesRun in its lifecycle — I need to check how completesRun actually behaves, whether it only matters when requireCompletionTool is set, and whether a failed save returning ok:false (rather than throwing) would incorrectly end the run. Throwing on failure seems safer since it becomes an error result instead of a \"successful\" completion, and I'd want sensible retry defaults like three retries.\n\nI'm also realizing throwing could trigger wasteful identical retries since the runtime retries tool execution on throw by default, so it's probably better to set retryable: false and return plain {ok:false} objects instead of throwing. I'm considering skipping completesRun entirely and instead relying on the system prompt instructing the agent to summarize and stop after a successful saveProposal, possibly backed by an afterTool hook that can signal a stop.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the runToolAgent wrapper in ai.ts is structured: it checks config availability, hashes the prompt, creates the agent through an injectable factory, subscribes to events to forward simplified updates, and runs the agent — returning the output on completion, retrying once on failure, and surfacing an ai_failed error if both attempts fail, with logging capturing the model, prompt version, hash, attempt, and outcome.\n\nRetrying the whole agent run could duplicate a saved proposal if partial progress happened, so I want to make retry conditional on a `shouldRetry` callback that checks whether a proposal was already saved before retrying.\n\nI'm also worried about importing @cline/sdk directly in ai.ts since it pulls in @cline/core and a lot of heavy dependencies (hub, sqlite stores, protobufjs) that could complicate Turbopack bundling for the Next build — I'm considering marking these as server external packages to avoid bundling issues.\n\nSince these packages are ESM-only, I should lazy-load them via dynamic import inside runToolAgent so the route module and tests don't eagerly load them, and mock the agent factory in vitest so tests skip the import entirely. I'm now checking whether the tools module's static import of @cline/sdk causes any side effects like telemetry calls during unit tests.\n\nRather than having tools statically import createTool, I'll define tool specs as plain objects with name, description, schema, and execute logic in a pure module, then convert them to actual SDK tools lazily inside ai.ts's runToolAgent via dynamic import. This lets tool tests call execute directly while ai.ts tests inject a fake SDK loader with mock Agent and createTool implementations.\n\nI'm also thinking through whether the SDK pulls in telemetry by default -- it shouldn't if left undefined -- and sketching the streaming API route that pushes NDJSON events for tool calls and results to the UI.\n\nFor keeping summaries simple, I want each tool's output to carry a precomputed `summary` string, so the event log mapper can just surface `output.summary` directly instead of reimplementing logic in the UI.\n\nI also want assistant-message text parts shown as \"note\" lines in the log. For the admin diff view, I'm thinking I'll compute diffs server-side by finding the latest published version of the same scheme with a lower version number than the current row, treating that as the base for comparison regardless of status—this handles pending, published, and rejected rows uniformly. Then the readable diff renderer humanizes field paths and shows before/after values, with objects collapsed into compact JSON.\n\nFor value formatting I'll keep a small pure helper—`formatDiffValue`—in the ruleDiff module that converts objects into readable key-value lines (handling nested provenance as a single summarized line), and I'll define a server-side view model with fields like id, schemeId, and related metadata for the admin UI to consume.\n\nI'm sketching the admin API: a GET endpoint returning schemes with their versions plus pending rule versions, and a publish endpoint that re-runs tests before publishing, returning a 409 with the report if tests fail. I'm also noting that there's no authentication for this hackathon version—flagging it as an open issue, possibly addressed later with an optional admin token.\n\nFor the citizen-facing flow, I need the homepage to sync the rule registry from the database at request time, wrapping the catalog in a Suspense boundary with an async component that triggers the sync before rendering. The check API route also needs to sync the registry before evaluating a scheme, but since tests shouldn't hit a real database, I'll make the DB path injectable via an environment variable so tests can point to an in-memory database instead.\n\nIf the database sync fails due to permissions or other issues, I should wrap it in a try/catch so built-in scheme checks still work offline. I also need to make sure the catalog picks up new schemes correctly and that any AI-generated Assamese translations are flagged as drafts rather than treated as verified content.\n\nFor the agent setup, I'm thinking a prompt version tag, a max iteration cap around 24, and a reasonable timeout per tool call around 30 seconds given typical model turn latency.\n\nFor testing, I want offline unit tests against an in-memory store covering schema retrieval, current rule lookup including regression tests, handling unknown schemes, validation failures and version bumps in propose, and save logic that enforces passing tests, version bumps, and notice URLs for verified status. Then I'd add a full integration test using a scripted fake agent that walks through the tool sequence end-to-end, checking emitted events, proposal persistence, and that publishing correctly updates the registry.\n\nI'm also planning the demo fixtures needed for the two sample proposals, likely as a test-data file excluded from the vitest glob but still type-checked by tsc, alongside tests for the tool-agent wrapper itself covering missing API key, retry-on-failure behavior, and logging of model/prompt/input metadata.\n\nFor the IGNOAPS fixture, I'm working out the expected agent output: id, versioning, bilingual names, confirmation authority, and benefit structure with a 200 central share. The tricky part is deciding verification status—since the source notice cites a Vikaspedia URL (a government-affiliated portal), I need to reason about whether that satisfies the policy requiring an official URL for \"verified\" status rather than defaulting to \"provisional\".\n\nSince the benefit schema doesn't support age-tiered amounts (200 for 60-79, 500 for 80+), I need the agent to fall back on using the base 200 value plus a note documenting the 80+ increase, flagging this as a schema limitation rather than inventing a workaround. For the age range, I'm also noting that max age needs a concrete value since open-ended ranges aren't supported, so I'll pick a reasonable upper bound like 120.\n\nI'm sketching out the required documents and verification logic for the BPL household scheme, then mapping out test cases covering eligible and ineligible ages, and finally setting up a fixture clone for Orunodoi with a version bump and overridden top-up amount.\n\nFor the Orunodoi tests, I'm verifying the October-to-November top-up transition and the boundary date, making sure a regression test's date was adjusted so it remains valid after the override, and confirming the built-in tests at the October check date don't depend on benefit amounts. I'm also noting the need to check how the smoke-test script exercises both demo notices against the real API.\n\nSince .mjs can't easily import TS with path aliases, I'm deciding the smoke script should just hit the running server's API directly — POSTing to the rules-propose endpoint, streaming the NDJSON response, and printing tool calls and test results, consistent with the existing smoke script pattern. I'm also noting the SQLite database should live under a data directory relative to the working directory, creating it if missing.\n\nLet me quickly verify how AgentAfterToolResult and completesRun behave before writing the store along with its tests.\n\nThe demo's source line matches the regex, so I'll split the policy into rule-level and test-level checks since proposeRule only needs the rule part. Next, I'll check the SDK's after-tool hook result type.\n\n"}}
