{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:33:50.362Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Now I'm designing the store module: a function to open a SQLite store at a given path or in-memory, with a default singleton database under a gitignored data directory (overridable via env var), plus a rule_versions table schema tracking scheme ID, version, rule JSON, test JSON, and test results.\n\nI'm also working through uniqueness constraints for scheme+version pairs -- duplicate proposals with the same version should be allowed, but publishing should enforce only one published row per scheme+version, with the losing duplicate failing with an \"already published\" error. For built-in schemes, I'm deciding how to seed the initial published version so it represents the first entry in each scheme's version history.\n\nFor regression tests, I'm treating the set of tests for a scheme as the union of tests from all its published versions, which means a version that intentionally changes behavior could fail older tests -- a real limitation worth flagging, unless I allow the agent to retire outdated tests when publishing a breaking change.\n\nI'm deciding the seeding logic belongs in the rules service rather than the store, since it needs to run tests, while the store itself just exposes an `ensureSeeded` method that inserts default rows for schemes with no existing versions, wrapped in a transaction. For the actual SQLite layer I'm using node's built-in `DatabaseSync` with explicit BEGIN/COMMIT transactions and WAL journal mode, keeping in mind WAL doesn't apply to in-memory databases.\n\nNow I'm thinking through build-time concerns: `node:sqlite` should bundle fine as a Node builtin under Turbopack, but since the root page reads the DB, it needs to be dynamic—calling `connection()` first and wrapping in Suspense so cacheComponents treats it correctly, avoiding any database access during prerendering. I'm also considering that route handlers like the admin rules GET endpoint aren't prerendered, so they can safely touch the database at request time.\n\nI need to be careful that GET handlers call `connection()` before any synchronous DB access, since otherwise the prerender could execute the query directly. I'll keep that logic in the thin route files themselves rather than in the shared handler module, so tests can import and call the handler functions directly without hitting Next's request-context requirements.\n\nSince module instances could potentially be duplicated across route bundles in Next, the safest approach is to sync the registry from the database on every request — checking if each scheme/version is already registered before adding it, which is cheap given the small row count.\n\nI need to verify the \"newest wins\" semver resolution won't be polluted by test runs, since those should pass the rule object directly without registering it. Old published versions stay queryable through the registry's history, and I should make sure the sync call happens before any getScheme lookup in the check handler.\n\nFor the test database, I'll want to avoid creating a real file on disk during tests — pointing the store path to an in-memory database via an environment variable configured in vitest seems like the cleanest approach, keeping with the rule that only the store module touches the database directly.\n\nNow I'm sketching the rule maintenance agent module, which will expose a handful of tools plus session state tracking the last validated rule, test report, and saved proposal id.\n\nI'm defining getRuleSchema to return the JSON schema for scheme rules and test cases along with examples and enums, getCurrentRule to fetch an existing rule by scheme id or list available schemes if not found, and proposeRule to validate a submitted rule and return either a diff summary or validation errors.\n\nSince createTool likely won't enforce strict runtime validation beyond the shape, I'm making the rule input loosely typed as a generic record and instead running a manual safeParse inside execute so I can surface clean validation errors to the model rather than letting it throw.\n\nFor runTests, I'll parse both the rule and test cases, run a policy check, then execute new and regression tests to build a report. For saveProposal, I want the code itself—not the model's claims—to verify the rule is valid, passes policy, and that every test (new and regression) succeeds before storing it with status \"proposed\" and the test results; it returns either an ok result with the proposal id or a list of errors, and I'm deciding whether to allow only one save per run or let the model retry after fixes.\n\nI'm also figuring out what to store alongside sourceNote—maybe a separate notice_text column for the actual notice content, even though the spec only mentions sourceNote, since it could help with review without exposing personal data. I'm also considering a base_version column to support diffing against the published version at proposal time.\n\nFor age-banded benefits like IGNOAPS (₹200/month for 60-79, ₹500/month for 80+), the schema doesn't support age-dependent amounts directly, so I'm thinking the agent should set a broad ageRange with the base centralShare and add a note explaining the higher amount for 80+.\n\nFor document requirements I'm noting aadhaar, BPL proof, and bank passbook should be flagged provisional since the source citation needs verification.\n\nFor IGNOAPS test cases, I'm checking that age 65 maps to likely_eligible, age 55 to not_eligible verified, and benefit amount of 200. I also need to make sure new schemes become selectable dynamically after publishing through the scheme catalog, and that AI-generated Assamese translations get flagged as drafts in the admin UI for review.\n\nI'll pass an optional set of draft IDs keyed by id and version into the schemeView builder, and add a badge in SchemeStep flagging Assamese text as AI-drafted, with i18n keys for both languages. This would also apply to the Orunodoi update since its Assamese text carries over from the agent, which seems acceptable since it's still AI-drafted content.\n\nNow for the agent runner itself, following the rule that every model call must go through ai.ts with structured JSON output, schema validation, one retry, and a safe fallback while logging the model, prompt version, and input hash — I'm defining a `runToolAgent` function with a spec covering the prompt version, system prompt, input, available tools, max iterations, a completion check, and event/cancellation hooks, returning either a completed result with output text and attempt count or an error state.\n\nFor the retry logic, I'm deciding that if a run fails outright due to a provider error, I retry with a fresh run, but if it completes without being \"done,\" I continue the same agent with a nudge message instead of wasting a fresh attempt — either way counts as the single allowed retry. The tool calls themselves are zod-validated structured output, while the agent's final text summary is never trusted for decisions, only used as a human-readable summary.\n\nFor testability, I'm adding a dependency injection point so tests can supply a fake agent implementing run/continue/subscribe, letting me simulate the model calling tools in sequence to exercise real tool logic. I'm also weighing whether importing the full SDK is worth the load time and bundling risk versus importing a lighter agents-only package, given the project's stated install requirement.\n\nVitest should import @cline/sdk fine as an ESM package, and I don't expect problematic side effects like telemetry or filesystem writes on import — the earlier probe ran cleanly. I'll go with @cline/sdk as the direct dependency rather than the transitive @cline/agents, and mark it as a server external package so Node resolves it natively.\n\nBut there's a real risk around zod instance mismatch: since @cline/sdk is external but our own code bundles zod separately, `instanceof` checks against ZodType could fail across the boundary, causing schemas to be passed raw instead of converted to JSON schema. I need to make sure schema conversion happens safely regardless of which zod instance is in play.\n\nAlso I'm realizing embedding the full SchemeRule schema into each tool's input definition would bloat token usage significantly across proposeRule, runTests, and saveProposal, so I should reference it more compactly instead.\n\nBut a bigger problem: repeating the full rule JSON across three tool calls is slow and expensive at demo speed. Better to keep session state where proposeRule stores the last valid rule as a draft, and runTests/saveProposal just operate on that draft without resending the whole thing.\n\nI'm also considering making `rule` and `tests` optional in those signatures, defaulting to the last proposeRule/runTests call, which keeps spec compatibility while saving tokens. For something like Orunodoi where Assamese tokenizes poorly and the rule is large, I could also let proposeRule accept a patch against the current rule instead of requiring the full JSON every time.\n\nA JSON merge patch (RFC 7386) applied to the currently published rule would work well here, since arrays like overrides just get replaced wholesale, making it compact. I'd implement a pure, tested applyMergePatch in core, and let proposeRule's input take either `rule` (for new schemes) or `patch`+`schemeId` (for existing ones) -- with validation returning errors, a diff summary, or policy violations, storing the draft in session state on success.\n\nFor runTests, I'll keep it simple with `{ testCases, rule? }`, defaulting to the current draft when no rule is given, and reserve patch support just for proposeRule to avoid extra complexity. saveProposal then defaults to the draft and last run tests, but always re-validates and reruns everything before committing, refusing to save on any failure or policy violation.\n\nI'm thinking the model should write at least three test cases, and getRuleSchema should return the full JSON schema plus an example so the model understands the shape. For tool call sizing, getCurrentRule for something like orunodoi-3 returns roughly 10KB of rule and test data, which is manageable. I'll cap iterations around 16, tag the prompt version as \"rule-agent-v1\", and derive the input hash from a truncated SHA256 of the notice — I also need to handle the case where the route handler request gets aborted mid-run.\n\nFor abort handling, I'll wire the agent's own abort() alongside a four-minute overall timeout that triggers it. The proposal endpoint will stream NDJSON events — start, tool-started/finished with name and summary, optional text, and done with proposalId or error — read client-side via a stream reader. Rather than parsing SDK events generically, I'll have each tool generate its own human-readable summary directly in its implementation.\n\nEach tool's execute function emits a started event with input summary and a finished event with result summary, decoupled from SDK internals and easy to test for the live log. I'll also pass through assistant-message text as a lightweight \"note\" event, mapped in the route handler by joining text parts—shown to the user but not used for decision logic.\n\nI'm leaving /admin unprotected for the hackathon demo but flagging it as a real risk since the publish endpoint lets anyone change rules; an optional ADMIN_TOKEN env check would be the minimal fix if time allows. For persistence, I'll store the SQLite file under data/kaagaz.db and gitignore that directory (though *.db is already covered). I also need to make sure the build doesn't touch the database—calling connection() lazily inside the admin rules route and letting the homepage stop prerendering at that call, while the admin page just fetches client-side as a static shell.\n\nWith cacheComponents enabled, calling connection() for dynamic data outside a Suspense boundary will cause a build error, so I need to wrap the async component that reads from the database in a Suspense boundary with a simple loading fallback on the homepage.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the rule registry service: a function to seed built-in rules and register any published versions missing from the registry, a function to list rule versions with diffs for the admin view, and a function to fetch the current published rule plus its union of regression tests for a given scheme.\n\nI also need validation and test-running functions, plus proposal workflows: saving a proposal runs validation and tests before storing, publishing re-verifies the version ordering and re-runs tests atomically within a transaction before registering, and rejecting simply updates status.\n\nKeeping the store as the sole writer, I'll give it a `publishVersion` method that accepts a guard callback run inside the transaction against the row and the scheme's other published rows, returning an error string or null -- concurrency across processes isn't something I need to worry about here. Now I'm sketching the store's row schema and API, defining the rule version row shape and a `seedBuiltins` method for inserting default rows.\n\nThe `origin` column will drive the \"AI draft\" UI badge, and I'll let the service compute and pass in ISO timestamps rather than having the store generate them. For testing, I need coverage of the store's seed/insert/list/publish/reject flows including guard-triggered rollbacks and double-publish rejection, plus tool-level tests that exercise an in-memory store-backed service directly.\n\nI also want an end-to-end fake agent test simulating the full propose-then-publish flow, confirming the registry and catalog update correctly. Since the module registry is shared, I need to be careful about test isolation—using unique scheme ids per test or checking for existing registration before re-registering to keep things idempotent, and wiring a `createRulesService(store)` factory so tests can inject an in-memory store instead of the default singleton.\n\nFor handlers, I'll default to a lazily-created `getRulesService()`. The default DB path resolves from `process.cwd()`, so for tests I should set `KAAGAZ_DB_PATH: \":memory:\"` in the vitest env to avoid creating a real file during test runs. I'm also checking that nothing touches the database at build time—pages and routes only query on actual requests, not during prerender—and confirming `node:sqlite` works fine as a builtin, verifying Turbopack doesn't complain about it.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the version history UI and diff tooling, plus how benefit overrides should display on the citizen-facing side—base benefit values versus override notes—and deciding to skip showing the override distinctly for now. I'm also checking that the client resends the scheme version after a publish so pages pick up the new state, and sketching out the single-client-component admin app structure, starting with the header and language considerations.\n\nI'm weighing whether admin UI strings should live alongside citizen strings in the shared i18n file with both Assamese and English translations, or whether to keep the admin interface English-only since maintainers are technical users—leaning toward English-only admin strings to avoid doubling translation work, while still keeping them organized under an admin section in the i18n file.\n\nFor the admin layout, I'm sketching a scheme-notice card with a textarea and demo-load buttons for an Old Age Pension and Orunodoi notice, plus a primary \"Run agent\" action, with the demo text pulled from markdown files in docs/ read server-side.\n\nBelow that I want a live agent-log feed showing status icons, tool names, and summaries alongside the assistant's notes, and once the run finishes, refreshing the rules list and auto-selecting the new proposal to display in a ProposalCard with a title, source note, and a diff table comparing before/after values.\n\nIt should also show test results with pass/fail marks, origin badges distinguishing new tests from regressions, and failure messages, plus Publish/Reject buttons visible only while the proposal is pending — publishing triggers a refresh and a confirmation linking back to the citizen flow. Below that, a version history section grouped per scheme listing version number, status badge, creation date, and origin (built-in vs agent), clickable for details.\n\nThe admin API endpoint returns each scheme with its current version and a list of version views containing id, status, origin, source note, timestamps, base version, diff of rule changes, test report, and name. Diffs are computed against the base version's rule definition pulled from the store or registry, and since diff values may be objects I'm planning a small pure helper to format them into readable key-value lines, likely living alongside the rule diff logic as a `describeValue` function.\n\nFor formatting arbitrary diff values, I'm designing a recursive function that renders strings and numbers as-is, nulls as \"none\", arrays as dashed list items, and objects as indented key-value lines, with the total output truncated around 1200 characters and displayed in a pre-formatted block with wrapping. I'm also considering a separate pure module for building agent log summaries that tools can populate.\n\nNow I'm turning to the plan for testing each pure module starting with mergePatch, and thinking through how a patch missing a version field would be rejected by the \"version must be higher\" policy check, so I need the model to supply defaults. Patches should apply to the parsed current rule's JSON representation, then re-validate with the schema, after which I'll build out mergePatch, the store, the rules service, and the agent tool wiring in sequence.\n\n"}}
