{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:46:23.516Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I realize syncRegistry just needs to be idempotent if the version's already registered by the same-process publish call, which still validates the behavior I want. Now I'm moving on to writing rules.ts, starting with a Result type that captures either success with data or failure with an error list.\n\nI'm sketching the RuleService class: syncRegistry registers any published row not yet in the scheme registry, currentRule fetches a scheme's active rule post-sync, and regressionTests collects builtin plus published-version test cases. I'm also outlining parseRule for schema parsing, validate for combining zod validation with policy checks, runTests for validating and executing test cases against a rule, saveProposal for persisting a validated proposal, and publish for re-validating policy and tests before marking a row published and registering the scheme.\n\nI'm reasoning through edge cases: regression tests for a brand-new unpublished scheme should be empty, checkTestPolicy prevents duplicate test ids across published versions, and registerScheme should throw only on duplicate id+version pairs which the publish policy already guards against by requiring version increments. For publish, I want to confirm the scheme isn't already registered before calling store.setStatus, then register it afterward so the registry stays consistent with the database state. I'm also settling on a zod error format of `path: message` capped to roughly twenty entries, and starting to think about whether the proposal tool should support merge patches.\n\nNow I'm deciding where patch resolution should live -- either as a RuleService helper or within the agent tools layer, with a function that builds a candidate rule by applying a merge patch onto the current rule for a scheme when a full rule object isn't supplied. I'm weighing whether to expose the full SchemeRule schema as the tool's input type so the model gets proper validation guidance.\n\nI'm leaning toward using a loose zod record type for rule/patch at the tool boundary since the SDK doesn't validate inputs itself, while still exposing the full schema separately via a dedicated schema-retrieval tool so the model can introspect the expected shape.\n\nGood, the shared package's zod version is hoisted to a single instance so instanceof checks against ZodType work correctly, which confirms the earlier JSON schema generation was accurate. Time to move on and start writing rules.ts.\n\n"}}
