{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:49:26.249Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm designing a tool agent gateway function that takes a spec with the function name, prompt version, system prompt, input message, available tools, iteration limits, and a completion check to drive the agent loop.\n\nNow I'm weighing dependency choices: injecting an agent factory defaulting to @cline/sdk versus the lighter @cline/agents package, since @cline/sdk pulls in the full core stack and could complicate the Next.js server build. I'm leaning toward marking these packages as external in next.config and dynamically importing @cline/sdk inside ai.ts so it stays out of test bundles.\n\nFor ruleMaintenance.ts, I still need createTool from the SDK to define tools, but I'm worried about whether loading @cline/core at import time triggers side effects like telemetry or filesystem writes that could affect test isolation — though my probe suggests it's likely safe since createTool is just a thin zod-to-schema converter.\n\nI'm also checking that vitest handles the ESM-only @cline/sdk package correctly, and confirming the zod instance used for schema validation resolves consistently to avoid instanceof mismatches across nested dependencies.\n\nSince it's unclear whether the runtime itself validates tool input against the schema, I'll keep execute lenient by validating with zod's safeParse internally and returning errors as output rather than throwing, so the model can self-correct. For the proposeRule tool, I'll keep the top-level inputSchema loose (unknown/record fields) rather than exposing the full 14KB schema at the JSON-schema level, since that's likely too large for strict tool-call mode, and instead validate the detailed SchemeRule shape inside the handler.\n\nNow I'm designing a getRuleSchema tool that returns the JSON schema for SchemeRule and RuleTestCase along with vocabulary notes and a compact example — likely using the small nsap-widow rule as the illustrative case since it keeps token size reasonable. I'm weighing whether to also add a listSchemes tool, though that's not explicitly called for in the spec.\n\nSince the spec only lists five tools (getRuleSchema, getCurrentRule, proposeRule, runTests, saveProposal), I'll fold the existing scheme list into the user prompt instead, and have getCurrentRule return `{found:false, existingSchemes:[...]}` when given an unknown id so the agent can still distinguish new schemes from edits. I'm also sketching proposeRule's return shape, covering both success (with ruleVersion and a diff summary) and failure cases.\n\nFor runTests, I'll let the input omit rule/patch and fall back to the last valid proposed rule from the session, keeping responses compact by only detailing failures while just listing ids for passes. saveProposal will similarly default to the stored proposed rule if none is given explicitly, alongside tests, a summary, and a source note.\n\nI'm now weighing whether a successful saveProposal call should mark the run as complete via a `completesRun` lifecycle flag — but \"successful\" likely just means the call didn't throw, which could prematurely end the run even if the output signals `ok:false`. So instead, I should throw an Error on validation failure so it surfaces as an isError response and the run continues, reserving `completesRun: true` for genuine success cases.\n\nBut since tools default to `retryable: true` with up to 3 retries, throwing on deterministic failures would trigger wasteful auto-retries — I should set `retryable: false` on all tools. For proposeRule and runTests, since they don't carry `completesRun`, I can just return a structured `{ ok: false, errors }` instead of throwing.\n\nI also want to confirm how `completesRun` behaves in the runtime — whether throwing inside it actually ends the run as \"completed\" versus some other status, since I'm considering using it only for saveProposal while keeping propose/runTests purely object-returning. As an alternative I wondered about using `afterTool`'s `stop` flag instead, but throwing with completesRun still seems like the cleaner mechanism, so I want to verify the runtime source before committing. On iteration limits, I'm thinking around 14 max, with the typical flow starting with parallel calls to fetch the rule schema and current rule.\n\nGiven the scale of a full rule authoring task (several KB of JSON for something like IGNOAPS), I'm estimating the model will need thousands of output tokens, which could slow iteration time considerably. I'm also weighing whether to let the agent call saveProposal directly rather than enforcing the propose→test→save sequence, and noting specific scheme details, like IGNOAPS's two-tier benefit structure (₹200 for ages 60-79, ₹500 for 80+), which doesn't map cleanly onto a single centralShare field in the existing SchemeRule benefit schema.\n\nI'm thinking through how to avoid inventing criteria not stated in the source: setting stateTopUp to null since the top-up contribution is only \"urged\" and unconfirmed, and figuring out how to represent an open-ended upper age bound (perhaps using 120 as a technical ceiling rather than a real eligibility criterion, with a system prompt hint for such cases). I'm also cataloging the required documents (aadhaar, bpl_proof, bank_passbook) and how provenance should trace back to the source URL.\n\nI'm weighing whether the vikaspedia source counts as \"official\" enough to mark verified, leaning toward it since it's a government-backed portal and the policy allows verification when a URL is present in the notice. I'm also deciding not to extend the schema for age-tiered benefits despite the 85-year-old test case showing a flat 200 central figure, since that would be scope creep -- instead just flagging it in notes. Finally, I'm framing the done-when condition as having a valid Old Age Pension scheme with at least three passing tests that becomes selectable once published.\n\nSeparately, after publishing, I need the scheme to register properly so listSchemes picks it up dynamically on the page. Since the agent writes Assamese labels itself, those are AI-generated and should be flagged per the trust rule -- I'm thinking the admin diff view should show a note that the Assamese text was drafted by AI and needs review, while keeping citizen-facing labeling simple, maybe just a small draft badge on agent-published scheme cards.\n\nFor the Orunodoi update to version 2.1.0, I'm verifying the test dates: since today is October 10, 2026, I need to make sure the regression test dated September 15, 2026 still behaves correctly under the new override, while confirming the October and November benefit amounts both compute as expected.\n\nThen I realize the override patch needs full provenance metadata—source title, clause, effective date, verification date, version, and status marked as provisional—and I'm wrestling with what date to use for verifiedOn since the agent itself doesn't inherently know today's date, so I'll pass it in explicitly.\n\nNow I'm shifting to designing the streaming API for proposing rule changes: a POST endpoint that accepts a notice and returns an NDJSON stream of events like status, tool-call, tool-result, assistant, done, and error, with the route handler creating a ReadableStream that pushes agent log lines and finishes with either the completed proposal (diff plus test results) or an error. I'm defining the shape of these agent log events next.\n\nFor retry logic, I'm implementing rule 3: one retry attempt with a fresh agent and a hint prefix if the first fails, then falling back to an error after both attempts, logging details like model, attempt number, outcome, and timing for each try. For testing runToolAgent, I'm planning to inject a fake `createAgent` with a minimal `ToolAgentLike` interface exposing `subscribe` and `run`.\n\nI also need an overall timeout (around 240s) that triggers `agent.abort()` via setTimeout, and a mocked-agent test for ruleMaintenance where a fake agent calls tools in sequence—getCurrentRule, proposeRule, runTests, saveProposal—to validate the full proposeRuleChange pipeline offline. The `notice` state lives in a session object passed into `createRuleMaintenanceTools`, alongside optional `proposed` and `savedId` fields.\n\nFor deployment config, I should externalize `@cline/*` packages via serverExternalPackages so Turbopack doesn't try to bundle the SDK—letting `ai.ts` dynamically import it at runtime safely. Since `ruleMaintenance.ts` statically imports from the SDK, I'll keep that import isolated to the propose route only, so other admin routes (like GET /api/admin/rules and publish) stay lightweight and don't pull in the whole SDK unnecessarily.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the admin page's server component—it reads from node:sqlite via rules.ts, which should be fine since Turbopack treats node builtins as external. The trickier issue is the home page: with cacheComponents enabled, calling getRuleService() synchronously during prerender could bake stale DB data into the build, so I likely need to call connection() first and wrap the data-dependent parts in Suspense to avoid a build error about uncached data accessed outside Suspense.\n\nFor demo notices, I'm thinking I could read the two docs/*.md files from disk at request time since the production server retains filesystem access, rather than bundling them at build. I'll likely expose this through the admin rules API or a dedicated endpoint, returning schemes, version history, and the demo notice contents together, keeping the admin page itself as a server component that just renders what's fetched.\n\nActually, using `readFileSync` synchronously in the server component lets these docs get baked into the prerendered static shell, which is cleaner — the page reads them at build/prerender time and passes them down to the client app, while the client separately fetches live history data. I should also flag in the log that these admin routes currently lack authentication, since this is just a hackathon demo but worth noting as an open issue.\n\nPer the rules, all AI/model calls must funnel through one module (src/server/ai.ts), so I'll put the tool-agent creation there as `runToolAgent`, with ruleMaintenance.ts just defining the tools and prompt and calling into it. I also want to verify whether importing @cline/sdk has side effects like writing to ~/.cline, and check how the runtime handles a completed run.\n\nStore and rule service tests are passing (7 + 11). Before building the agent layer, I'll check how `completesRun` behaves and whether importing `@cline/sdk` triggers file writes, to keep tests offline and clean.\n\n"}}
