{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:51:35.350Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Those writes in ~/.cline come from Cline itself, not my import, so that's fine. I'm now checking whether a tool with lifecycle.completesRun set to true would end the run once saveProposal succeeds, and whether that behavior is on by default or requires completionPolicy.requireCompletionTool—looking at findCompletingToolMessage to figure out where it's used.\n\nI'll go ahead and set completesRun true on saveProposal since createTool passes lifecycle through. For the generic runToolAgent in ai.ts, I'm sketching an interface for a tool agent request with a name, prompt version, system prompt, input text, tool list, max iterations, event callback, and an isDone function that lets code decide success criteria. The structured output here is really the tool input validated by zod, so a run succeeds when isDone() returns true (a proposal got saved); otherwise it retries once with a fresh agent, falling back to an error object if that fails too.\n\nI also need the Agent factory to be injectable for tests, with an AgentLike interface exposing run, subscribe, and abort. I'm planning to wire an AbortSignal plus a timeout that calls agent.abort() if an attempt runs too long. For importing the SDK, I want to avoid a static import bloating every route, so I'll use a dynamic import inside runToolAgent and mark the SDK packages as server-external so Next doesn't bundle them.\n\nFor createTool, importing it from @cline/sdk pulls in all of @cline/core, which is slower than the lighter @cline/agents re-export, but since @cline/agents isn't a direct dependency, I'll stick with importing createTool and Agent from @cline/sdk to stay consistent with the stack's declared dependencies.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach would be to define tool specs as plain objects with zod schemas in ruleMaintenance.ts, and let ai.ts handle the createTool call -- that way only ai.ts needs the SDK import, and the tools themselves can be unit-tested without pulling in @cline/core at all.\n\nFor execute error handling, I'll avoid throwing and instead return structured `{ ok: false, errors }` objects so the model gets usable feedback. Since I'm not sure createTool validates input against the schema automatically, I'll run `safeParse` manually inside each execute function and surface validation errors that way.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether to embed the full SchemeRule schema as the inputSchema directly—that's 14KB of JSON Schema sent on every call across multiple tools, which is wasteful and risks strict providers rejecting it. Instead, I'll expose a `getRuleSchema()` tool that returns it once, and keep the actual tool input types loose (an object record validated internally with zod), which still counts as schema-validated input while keeping requests lean.\n\nNext I'm designing `getCurrentRule({ schemeId })`, which either returns the found rule with its regression tests and versions, or signals it wasn't found along with a list of existing schemes so the agent can decide whether to create something new.\n\nI also want `proposeRule({ schemeId?, rule?, patch? })` to validate against zod and policy checks, returning either a diff summary or errors, and to cache the last valid candidate in session state so `runTests` can reference it by default without resending the whole rule.\n\nFor `saveProposal`, I'll let both `rule` and `tests` be optional, falling back to the session's last proposed rule and last test run, keeping explicit overrides possible but minimizing token overhead for the common path.\n\nFor `runTests`, the output should return pass/fail counts plus per-test results, trimmed to show failure details only while passed tests are just listed by id to keep the payload small.\n\nNow I'm thinking about session state design — tracking the notice, last proposed rule, last tests, saved version, and event log per agent run so later tool calls can reference prior context implicitly.\n\nI also need to handle a new scheme case: IGNOAPS, which pays different pension amounts for two age bands (60-79 vs 80+), but the current SchemeRule model only supports a single benefit and one age range. I'm weighing whether to model this with a broader age range plus an explanatory note, since splitting into two separate rules might be cleaner but adds complexity.\n\nSince max is a required field in the age range schema, I'm considering making it optional to represent open-ended ranges like \"60 or above\" without inventing an arbitrary upper bound, which seems like a small, low-risk change given how the age check logic uses it.\n\nI'll make max optional rather than inventing a fake cap—it's a small, testable change. For the 80+ benefit tier, I'll keep the base amount with a note flagging that the age-banded ₹500 increase isn't modeled yet, logging it as a follow-up item. Mandatory docs—Aadhaar, BPL proof, bank passbook—already map cleanly to the DocType fields.\n\nI'm also checking that the new scheme becomes selectable once published, since the listing page needs to be dynamic, and verifying the BPL verification rule is satisfied since the notice cites a sourceUrl. I'll flag the agent-written Assamese translation as a draft in my summary since it's AI-generated and unreviewed.\n\nFor the Orunodoi patch, I'm adding a benefit override with no sourceUrl since the notice lacks a citable URL, marking it as provisional provenance instead — confirming the nullable schema allows that and that the engine/UI correctly surfaces the provisional status on the benefit card.\n\nThe version bump field is minor, not worth worrying about. I'm checking that the patch preserves all existing verified provenances unchanged while adding the new provisional override entries, keyed by array index since they lack ids. Running the built-in regression tests confirms the date I kept for oru_benefit_1250 still resolves to 1250 as expected.\n\nNow moving to event streaming for the UI, I'm defining the AgentLogEvent union type covering status updates, tool calls, tool results, assistant text, and completion events.\n\nWriting the event type definitions...\n\nI'm deciding each tool should build its own human-readable summary from its inputs and outputs rather than parsing generic SDK events — things like \"7/7 passed\" for tests or \"proposal #4 saved\" for saves. I prefer having tools emit log events directly since it's deterministic and testable without depending on the SDK's tool-started/tool-finished lifecycle.\n\nFor the route, I'll have `POST /api/admin/rules/propose` take a `{ notice }` body and return a streamed NDJSON response via a ReadableStream, with the client reading it back using fetch's reader and TextDecoder, splitting on newlines.\n\nThe final \"done\" event carries the proposal view, including a server-computed diff and test results, and I should build a shared `proposalView` helper so the GET list endpoint can reuse it. That GET endpoint returns schemes with their version history (built-in plus published plus store rows) and the list of proposals, which the admin page fetches on mount.\n\nFor the demo notice text, I'm reading the markdown files directly with `readFileSync` in the server component rather than exposing a separate API route -- that's simple and safe since the docs are static and get baked in during prerender.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the citizen-facing homepage: since it statically renders the catalog at build time, published updates won't show up after the fact. I need to make it dynamic by calling `connection()` before fetching the rule service and catalog so it re-evaluates on each request under cacheComponents.\n\nThat means wrapping the dynamic part in a Suspense boundary, with a static fallback shell and an async Catalog component that awaits the connection internally. I'm also noting that `/api/check` depends on the same registry sync before calling `getScheme`, which pulls in the sqlite store — so tests will need a separate `KAAGAZ_DB_PATH` pointing to a temp file instead of the real database.\n\nSetting that in `vitest.config.mts` as `:memory:` seems cleaner, since the store already handles that case by skipping directory creation. But then I realize a bigger issue: since the registry is just an in-memory module-level object, Next's server bundling could give route handlers and pages separate module instances, meaning a publish via the API wouldn't be visible to the page's registry — which is exactly why `syncRegistry()` needs to re-read from the database on every request rather than trusting the in-memory state alone.\n\nEach entry point — pages, the check route, admin routes — calls `getRuleService()`, which syncs from SQLite each time, so the DB stays the authoritative source regardless of which module instance is running. Multiple connections to the same file across instances should be fine given WAL mode and busy_timeout, and since writes only go through the one store module, there's no real contention; the sync cost itself is trivial, just a single SELECT plus JSON parsing. Now I need to wire `getRuleService()` into `handleCheck` in api.ts and make sure `node:sqlite` is treated as an external Node built-in during Turbopack bundling.\n\nI'm also thinking through the agent run timing: a full rule-proposal cycle likely takes several model turns, each a few seconds, with the heavier JSON output possibly pushing total runtime to 30-60 seconds, so I need to set generous iteration and timeout limits and plan for retry logic if a run fails to produce a saved proposal.\n\nFor retries, I want to pass the previous attempt's errors as a hint so the fresh agent run has context without overcomplicating the input. I'm also extending the logging schema to capture agent run details like iterations and new outcome types, and now sketching out the type definitions for agent injection in the ai module.\n\nWriting type definitions...\n\nFor the default factory I'm planning to dynamically import the SDK and wrap each tool spec before constructing the agent, and I need a helper that erases the generic Zod schema type so the tools array can hold heterogeneous specs safely.\n\nSince the SDK passes raw JSON to execute, I'm adding a pure `callTool` helper that validates input against the Zod schema and only runs the spec's execute on success, returning structured errors otherwise -- this wrapper is what the factory uses and what tests can exercise directly.\n\nNow I'm moving on to writing the rule maintenance module and defining its prompt version constant.\n\nI'm thinking through whether session state should persist across retry attempts. If attempt one leaves a stale rule proposal in memory, attempt two could mistakenly rely on it instead of generating a fresh one, so I need to decide whether to reset the session per attempt or guard against this confusion explicitly.\n\nNow I'm settling on passing tools as a factory function that rebuilds state fresh for each attempt, with isDone checking a shared saved variable outside the factory. Then I'm starting to define the ProposalView interface with an id and schemeId field.\n\nFor the diff computation, I decide the \"previous\" version should be the highest registered version below the row's version, pulled from a registry that includes built-in and published versions—this works uniformly whether the row is pending or already published. For the admin UI, I'm planning a proposal card showing the summary and a diff table formatting before/after values.\n\nI also want a readable diff format with human-friendly path labels mapping technical paths to plain descriptions, and a pretty-printed JSON view with scroll truncation for large objects.\n\nI'm also thinking about the lack of auth on admin endpoints -- it's a real risk if deployed, though the problem statement doesn't mention it. Rather than building a full auth system, I'll just flag this as a note for follow-up instead of adding scope creep.\n\nFor rate limiting, I'll add a simple in-process guard so only one agent run happens at a time, returning a 409 if a run is already in progress -- cheap protection against double-clicks.\n\nNow I'm drafting the system prompt: defining the agent's role as a Rule Maintenance Agent that works only through tools and never writes files directly, and outlining the steps -- read the notice, decide if it's an existing scheme or new one, fetch the current rule or schema accordingly, then propose the smallest possible change, preferring a JSON merge patch for existing schemes.\n\nI'm also specifying versioning rules (minor bump for patches, \"1.0.0\" for new schemes with kebab-case ids), strict provenance requirements for every changed item (source URL, title, clause, effective date, verification status that defaults to \"provisional\" unless an official URL is given), and handling time-bounded payments through benefit overrides with inclusive date ranges.\n\nI should never invent details not present in the notice—anything unclear goes into a provisional notes entry instead. I also need to write at least three new unique test cases covering eligible and ineligible scenarios with fake documents, then run the test suite to confirm everything checks out.\n\nRegression tests must never be altered to force a pass—if one fails, that signals my rule is wrong, not the test. Once everything passes, I'll call saveProposal with a concise sourceNote and summary explaining what changed and what remains provisional, since only a human reviewer can approve publishing. Assamese-language fields should use proper Assamese script with correct characters like ৰ and ৱ for native-speaker review.\n\nI also want to make sure the notice text, which is untrusted input prone to prompt injection, gets clearly fenced off and labeled as data rather than instructions—though the tools themselves enforce policy regardless. For getRuleSchema, I'm thinking the output should bundle the scheme rule schema, test case schema, check-id patterns, and a full worked example like the nsapWidow rule, totaling roughly 25 KB which seems like an acceptable token cost. I'm now weighing whether getCurrentRule should also surface the test case schema so the agent doesn't need a separate call to understand the format.\n\nRather than duplicating the full schema, I'll have getCurrentRule return existing regressionTests as de facto examples, supplement with a brief description of the test case format in the system prompt, and keep tool descriptions tight—accepting that the agent might still call getRuleSchema if needed. I'm also sketching out the output shapes for proposeRule (confirmation with a diff summary), runTests (pass/fail counts and failure details), and saveProposal (confirmation message noting a person must review the proposal).\n\nEach tool call should emit AgentLogEvent entries for both the call and result, handled through a shared logging wrapper inside ruleMaintenance. Since the event type is needed by both server and client UI, I'll place it in a shared core module along with any NDJSON parsing helpers the client streaming logic needs.\n\nFor the proposal view builder, since it needs both the row data and the previous version to diff against, I'll keep `toProposalView` in server/rules.ts where it has access to the registry, using diffRules under the hood.\n\nNow I should also check how `ageRange.max` being optional affects the widow pack logic and the UI that reads it.\n\nThe 11 tests for store, registry sync, publish, and reject pass. Before building the agent, I noticed the IGNOAPS \"60 years or above\" criterion has no upper bound, but `AgeRange.max` is required—so I'll check how `max` is used before deciding how to handle this without inventing a fake limit.\n\n"}}
