{"ts":"2026-10-10T10:54:26.886Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"For IGNOAPS, age 60+ has no upper limit, so I'll use max 120 for the age range. Since the benefit model can't express age-dependent amounts, I'll set centralShare to 200 (the base for 60-79) and note the 500 for 80+ separately, and leave stateTopUp null since state contributions are only \"urged,\" not confirmed.\n\nKeeping all AI calls routed through one module in ai.ts is important, so I'll design ruleMaintenance.ts to define tools and prompts while delegating the actual agent execution to a runToolAgent function in ai.ts. For retry logic, I want to avoid duplicating side effects like saved proposals, so I'll only retry once if the run failed and nothing was saved yet, with a fallback error response if AI is unavailable or fails entirely.\n\nFor logging, I'm tracking model, promptVersion, input hash, attempt number, outcome, duration, tool call count, iterations, and token usage. To keep things testable, I'll inject a createAgent factory through deps so tests can mock the agent runtime.\n\nI'm also thinking through the bundling concerns with importing the SDK—since it's heavy and pulls in native dependencies, I want to mark those packages as external and import them lazily inside runToolAgent, so routes and tests that don't need AI avoid loading it. The tool creation utility will need similar lazy handling to avoid pulling the heavy SDK into ruleMaintenance.ts statically.\n\nI'm now reconsidering whether to just rely on a static import from \"@cline/sdk\" for createTool rather than reaching into transitive dependencies like \"@cline/agents\", since that's not an explicit dependency and relying on hoisting would be fragile. I also want to verify createTool's zod-to-JSON schema conversion actually works correctly with our current zod version, since it relies on an instanceof check against a specific ZodType class.\n\nI'm checking whether createTool actually validates input against the zod schema at runtime or just converts it to JSON schema for the tool definition. Looking at the source, it seems execute is called directly with no zod parsing step, so inputs are unvalidated at the runtime level — meaning I should handle validation myself inside execute and keep the tool input schemas looser.\n\nSo I'm planning a loose JSON schema for the rule input (just an open-ended object) to avoid strict schema friction, with getRuleSchema exposing the real shape and example so the model understands the expected structure, and validation errors returned as output rather than thrown. The tool set is getRuleSchema, getCurrentRule, proposeRule, runTests, saveProposal, and I'm sketching a per-run RuleAgentSession to track the current candidate, test results, and save state.\n\nFor getRuleSchema's response I'm deciding to include the SchemeRule and RuleTestCase JSON schemas, a smaller example rule like nsap-widow rather than the bulkier orunodoi one, the list of existing scheme ids/versions so the model can tell whether it's creating new or modifying, and the naming conventions for check ids like doc_, name_, field_, age, and req_ prefixes.\n\nNow I'm designing the remaining tools: getCurrentRule to fetch an existing rule or report known schemes if not found, proposeRule to validate a candidate and store it in session state while returning a diff summary or errors, and runTests to execute test cases against either the provided rule or the last proposed candidate from the session.\n\nI need to work out saveProposal's completion semantics — whether a successful save should end the run automatically, and I'm realizing that a failed save (returning `{ok:false, errors}` without throwing) might incorrectly trigger that same completion logic since it's not technically an error output.\n\nSo for saveProposal, I should throw an actual Error on failure so the runtime marks it isError and continues the conversation instead of completing early. I also want to set `retryable: false` on all tools since the default auto-retry (up to 3 times) would be wasteful for deterministic failures like duplicate IDs — same input, same failure each time.\n\nFor the Old Age Pension scheme itself, I'm deciding on an id like \"nsap-old-age\" with version \"1.0.0\", and considering whether sources like vikaspedia count as \"official\" enough to mark fields verified rather than provisional, given it's a government-backed portal already used as a verified source elsewhere. For the scheme to actually be selectable after publishing, the catalog page needs to render published versions at request time.\n\nSince the root page is currently statically prerendered with `catalogOf(listSchemes())` at build time, I need to make it dynamic -- calling `connection()` and `getRuleService()` inside a Suspense boundary so the registry syncs from the DB on each request. With cacheComponents, uncached reads must be wrapped in Suspense or the build fails with a blocking-route error, so I'm restructuring page.tsx to wrap the catalog component in a Suspense boundary with a loading fallback.\n\nI also need `/api/check` to sync the registry before looking up published schemes, which means `handleCheck` would need to call `getRuleService()` too -- pulling the store and node:sqlite import into api.ts.\n\nTo keep tests from hitting the real database file in the project directory, I'm considering injecting a dependency for syncing rules into `handleCheck`, defaulting to the real service but allowing tests to override it so they don't open `data/kaagaz.db` during route testing.\n\nAlternatively, I'm thinking vitest could just set `KAAGAZ_DB_PATH` to `:memory:` in its config, with the RuleStore treating that as a special case and skipping directory creation, giving each test worker its own isolated in-memory database. I'm also worried about whether Next.js shares module state across route handlers and pages in the same server process, since different bundling layers might not guarantee a single shared module cache for the registry.\n\nThat's exactly why syncRegistry() exists as a call at every entry point -- the database is the real source of truth, and each module instance just re-syncs from it, which is robust against separate module instances. The sync is cheap since it's a single SELECT over published rows, and registerScheme only runs for schemes not already known locally.\n\nLooking at the catalog flow, listSchemes returns each scheme's newest version including the new \"nsap-old-age\" entry, and the app passes that version along to /api/check after sync, with sample filtering by schemeId naturally excluding anything tied to the new scheme. For benefit display, Orunodoi v2.1.0 still shows the base amount of 1250 since the override only appears in the report for an October as-of date -- and since the demo environment's date is set to October 10, 2026 in India, the report correctly shows ₹1,500 with the explanatory note.\n\nI'm now tracing the admin rules proposal endpoint, which streams NDJSON events covering start, tool start/end phases with names and summaries, and assistant text messages.\n\nThe final event includes either a proposal object or an error, and the UI consumes this via a fetch reader. I'm implementing this as a Next.js route handler returning a ReadableStream with an NDJSON content type on the Node runtime, since POST handlers are dynamic by default and there's no built-in timeout concern for long-running agent calls taking a few minutes. To avoid concurrent runs causing issues, I'm adding a simple in-process busy lock that returns 409 if another run is active. I'm also flagging that admin authentication isn't specified -- since /admin is open for this demo, I'm noting it as an open issue rather than implementing a token-based guard, unless an optional ADMIN_TOKEN env var is warranted.\n\nI'm sketching out a pure logging module with functions to summarize tool calls and results into short strings for the UI log, which I'll want to test. Then I'm moving into the main agent orchestration function that normalizes the notice, creates a session and toolset, and kicks off the tool-based agent run with the system prompt.\n\nThe run returns a proposal only if the session saved something, otherwise null, and I'm mapping the generic tool-agent events into tool-start, tool-end, and text event types for the UI.\n\nI need to trace how tool-end output and assistant text get extracted from the message content parts, matched by toolCallId. For the model, it's using CLINE_MODEL through an openai-compatible provider with a custom baseUrl, and I'm estimating maxIterations based on the expected sequence of calls: schema lookup, current rule fetch, propose rule, and test runs.\n\nAllowing for parallel tool calls per turn, I'll set maxIterations to 20 for safety. Each subsequent call resends the full conversation, so context grows to roughly 30-60k tokens per run, which seems acceptable, and I'll keep using tools rather than embedding the schema directly in the system prompt, just trying to keep the example compact. A full run generating a new scheme rule should take somewhere around 60-120 seconds.\n\nThe agent needs to draft Assamese text for fields like labelAs/textAs, which the system prompt should instruct, flagging these drafts for native review later. I also need to extend the AiLogEntry type to support a new \"runToolAgent\" function alongside \"extractDocument\", adding outcomes like \"failed\" and \"aborted\" and logging fields such as promptVersion, inputHash, attempt count, iterations, and toolCalls.\n\nFor enforcing structure, zod validation inside the tools covers the schema requirement, with one retry attempt and a fallback error object if everything fails. I'm designing an interface for the tool agent with run, subscribe, and abort methods, plus a factory function that creates agent instances from a runtime config.\n\nNow I need to verify which types the SDK actually exports — checking if AgentRuntimeEvent, AgentTool, and AgentRunResult are available from the core package's type declarations.\n\nI also need to think through the typecheck path since the SDK re-exports from core, which pulls in shared and llms packages, so everything needs to resolve cleanly under our tsconfig's bundler module resolution. Separately, I'm considering the abort/timeout mechanism for tool-running agents — setting a timer that calls abort after a few minutes — and noting the UI still needs to surface test results, both new and regression ones.\n\nFor the diff view, I'm working out that for each proposal row, the \"before\" state should be the latest published registry version lower than that row's version, computed server-side as a `changes` field in the API response; for a brand-new scheme there's simply no \"before.\" Diff values could be large objects like a whole new question, so the UI would render them as compact, truncated pretty-printed JSON, with paths humanized for readability.\n\nNow I'm sketching the API routes needed: a GET on rules that returns schemes with versions, summaries, diffs and test results, a propose POST, and publish/reject POSTs scoped to an id, all using Next's async params pattern for dynamic routes.\n\nFor the /api/check route, I'll make POST handlers always dynamic with an optional syncRules param defaulting to getRuleService(), tested via an in-memory DB path set through vitest config env. For the home page, I'll use connection() inside an async component wrapped in Suspense, while the admin page stays a client component with data fetched via GET requests rather than server-side, keeping the page static. For the demo doc-loading buttons, I'm considering reading the docs files at build time within a server component since fs reads there resolve statically.\n\nSince cacheComponents allows synchronous file reads to complete during prerendering, I can bake demo content directly into the static shell by reading files from the project root and passing them to AdminApp as a demos array — I'll note the admin interface stays in English despite the citizen-facing default being Assamese. I'm also planning to add the relevant SDK and core packages to serverExternalPackages in the Next.js config since the ai module depends on them.\n\nFor the ESM-only SDK package, Next.js handles ESM externals by using dynamic import at runtime, so the static import of createTool in ruleMaintenance should still work even though it loads the whole core package at route module evaluation — a brief one-time cost that's acceptable. I'm weighing whether to avoid that static import altogether by building tools dynamically instead.\n\nI'm also checking whether loading the SDK during tests triggers filesystem side effects like telemetry or config directories — the probe didn't show any, so that seems fine. Now I need to think through test scenarios for the evaluation agent, crafting fictional scheme documentation with eligibility rules based on age thresholds derived from date of birth.\n\nFor the benefit amount test, since I can't properly model the age-based payment tiers, I'll have the agent record a flat central share with null state top-up and null total as a conservative placeholder.\n\nNow I'm drafting the system prompt for the Rule Maintenance Agent, establishing that it can only interact through tools, never write files or code directly, and outlining the sequence of steps starting with calling getRuleSchema.\n\nThe first step is to call getRuleSchema once to learn the data formats, then decide whether the notice concerns an existing scheme or a brand new one — for existing schemes, propose the smallest possible JSON merge patch with an appropriate version bump, while new schemes get a short kebab-case id, version 1.0.0, and a full rule proposal. Every addition or change also needs provenance tracking back to the source URL and title from the notice.\n\nI need to make sure every claim traces back to the clause in the notice itself, mark status as \"provisional\" unless an official verifying URL exists, and never fabricate criteria or dates — anything unclear or unschematizable goes into notes with its own provenance instead. Finally, I should write at least three regression test cases with fake names and unique lowercase ids to validate the change.\n\nThen I'll call runTests to confirm both my new cases and all prior regression tests pass, fixing the rule rather than weakening any existing test if something breaks. Once proposeRule approves everything, I'll call saveProposal with a brief sourceNote and plain-English summary for human review, keeping Assamese draft fields in proper Assamese script, and pull today's date from the existing todayInIndia helper in api.ts rather than duplicating it.\n\nStill defining tool schemas...\n\nFor execute, I'll parse raw input with the zod schema internally using safeParse and surface validation errors rather than trusting the input directly.\n\nSession behavior: proposeRule validates a candidate against the scheme and, if valid, stores it on the session while returning a diff summary with a hint to run tests next; otherwise it returns the errors. runTests pulls the rule from input or falls back to the session's stored rule (erroring if neither exists), then runs the suite and stores results, returning pass/fail counts, failure details, and new case IDs.\n\nI also need to fold in policy warnings from checkTestPolicy, and build saveProposal to validate the rule (from input or session), gather tests, and persist via the service — succeeding with a \"proposed\" status or throwing a combined error message on failure. I should enforce one save per run by checking if session.saved already exists, and make sure tools never touch source files directly, only going through the service layer, plus define the UI event shape for the rule agent.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the user-facing log messages for each tool call, like \"Reading the rule format\" for getRuleSchema or \"Proposing a change...\" for proposeRule, so the agent's actions read clearly in the UI.\n\nI want these summarizers to live in `src/core/agentLog.ts` as pure, testable functions. Then I'm moving to `ai.ts`'s runToolAgent, checking what type exports are available from `@cline/sdk` for the agent runtime event.\n\n"}}
